A.eno=s(["Finding patterns in data and using them to predict","Reasoning from first principles like a philosopher","Following hand-written if/else rules","Consciously thinking through problems"],t.s)
A.agO=new B.ap("At its core, what is modern AI actually doing?",A.eno,0,'Modern AI is pattern matching at massive scale. Not sentient, not conscious, not reasoning \u2014 just really good at "this looks like that".')
A.eA8=s(["Classical machine learning","Generative AI","Rule-based expert systems","Regression models"],t.s)
A.aej=new B.ap("Which family of AI produces brand-new text, images, or audio rather than sorting existing content?",A.eA8,1,"Generative AI creates new content. Classical ML mostly labels or predicts on existing content. ChatGPT is generative; a spam filter is classical.")
A.dr2=s(["Memorising every example word-for-word","Downloading knowledge from the internet in real time","Adjusting internal weights to reduce prediction error","Voting between separate copies of itself"],t.s)
A.agI=new B.ap("A neural network learns by doing what during training?",A.dr2,2,"Training = show the model an example, measure how wrong it was, nudge the weights, repeat billions of times. That is literally the whole game.")
A.eYH=s(["Letters, one by one","Whole sentences as single units","Your intent, extracted directly","Tokens \u2014 chunks of characters that map to numbers"],t.s)
A.aeo=new B.ap("When you send a message to a chatbot, what does the model actually read?",A.eYH,3,"LLMs read tokens, not letters or words. A token is usually a short chunk (~4 chars). The model turns each into a number, then predicts the next one.")
A.bVm=s(["It sets the stage \u2014 the model conditions its reply on that setup","It unlocks a hidden smarter model","It bypasses the safety filters","It makes the model search the web"],t.s)
A.adZ=new B.ap("Why does giving a chatbot a clear role or context up front usually improve the answer?",A.bVm,0,"A system prompt or role-setter changes what the model expects to produce next. Same underlying model \u2014 very different output.")
A.f_S=s(["They are trying to deceive the user","They complete plausible text, they do not verify facts","They ran out of training data mid-sentence","They are being sarcastic"],t.s)
A.agm=new B.ap("Why do language models hallucinate \u2014 confidently making things up?",A.f_S,1,'LLMs predict the next likely token. They don\'t have a "true / false" flag \u2014 plausible-sounding wrong is still their default failure mode.')
A.er9=s(["Ask the same model to double-check itself","Check the answer against a known-good source or your own knowledge","Trust it if the model sounds confident","Rephrase the prompt and see if the answer changes"],t.s)
A.agc=new B.ap("Which of these is the quickest reliable sanity check on an AI answer that matters?",A.er9,1,"Asking the model to self-check doesn't help much \u2014 it will happily defend a wrong answer. Real verification means stepping outside the chat.")
A.bnv=s(["Replacing your voice entirely so you never have to edit","Judging what is emotionally true for your audience","Deciding what you personally believe","Getting a rough first draft or utility work done faster so you can edit"],t.s)
A.afo=new B.ap("Where does AI reliably add the most value in your own writing?",A.bnv,3,"AI is great for drafts, structure, and grammar. Voice, taste, and truth are still yours \u2014 treat the model as a first pass, not the final say.")
A.b0H=s(["Ask questions and follow up on what you did not understand","Ask it to write a full essay you can memorise","Trust its first explanation and stop there","Search the web instead \u2014 AI is unreliable"],t.s)
A.ael=new B.ap("What is the single most powerful move when learning a new topic with AI?",A.b0H,0,'The Socratic loop \u2014 ask, get an explanation, ask a sharper follow-up \u2014 is where AI beats a textbook. It patiently answers the "wait, but why?" you would never ask a teacher.')
A.ez8=s(["Fully replacing themselves so they never work again","The boring stuff \u2014 inbox triage, meeting notes, spreadsheet formulas, first-draft docs","Deciding company strategy for them","Reading their team's minds"],t.s)
A.age=new B.ap("Where do most people find the biggest early productivity win from AI at work?",A.ez8,1,"The reliable wins are the small ones stacked daily: draft this email, summarise this meeting, explain this formula. That is where hours actually come back.")
A.b4a=s(['Insisting "you MUST get this right" in all caps',"Giving no context so the model can be creative","Asking it to reason step by step before giving the final answer","Repeating the question three times"],t.s)
A.agg=new B.ap("Which technique reliably improves how a model handles tricky multi-step problems?",A.b4a,2,'Chain-of-thought \u2014 "think step by step" \u2014 measurably helps on reasoning tasks. Models generate better final answers when they generate the intermediate steps too.')
A.cAU=s(["Retrains the model on new data each morning","Compresses the model to run on a phone","Makes the model bigger so it knows more","Fetches relevant docs and hands them to the model at answer time"],t.s)
A.agz=new B.ap("What does Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) do?",A.cAU,3,'RAG = search + generate. The model does not "learn" your docs; it looks them up on the fly and answers with them in context. Cheaper than fine-tuning, fresher too.')
A.dud=s(["It can call tools, take actions, and loop on the results","It has more parameters than a chatbot","It runs on faster hardware","It never hallucinates"],t.s)
A.af9=new B.ap("What separates an AI agent from a plain chatbot?",A.dud,0,"An agent = a model + tools + a loop. It decides the next action, does it, sees what happened, decides again. Chatbots just talk; agents get stuff done (or fail loudly trying).")
A.cbG=s(["The model has multiple personalities to switch between","The model runs in multiple data centres at once","The model can handle more than one type of input \u2014 e.g. text, images, audio, video","The model was trained by multiple companies"],t.s)
A.af_=new B.ap('What does "multi-modal" mean when we talk about modern AI models?',A.cbG,2,"Multi-modal = one model, many input types. You can drop in a photo of a receipt, an audio clip, or a screenshot and ask questions across all of them.")
A.ePM=s(["Always \u2014 fine-tuning is strictly better","Never \u2014 fine-tuning is obsolete","Only when prompting and RAG hit a ceiling AND you have consistent, high-volume data","Whenever you want a slightly different tone"],t.s)
A.agV=new B.ap("For most everyday use cases, when should you reach for fine-tuning over just prompting or RAG?",A.ePM,2,"Prompting is cheap and instant. RAG is next. Fine-tuning is durable but expensive and slow to iterate \u2014 save it for when the cheaper tools genuinely stop working.")
A.ckK=s(["They use the most expensive model","They have a technical background","They type faster","They built a daily habit \u2014 a handful of tasks they always run through AI"],t.s)
A.afb=new B.ap("What separates people who get a huge productivity boost from AI at work from those who do not?",A.ckK,3,"The compounding win comes from habit, not novelty. Same three or four tasks (draft email, summarise meeting, unblock spreadsheet) run through AI every day is worth more than a monthly power session.")
A.dxm=s(["The training data \u2014 the model reproduces patterns that were in the examples","The model choosing to be biased","Randomness in the hardware","Bugs in the model weights"],t.s)
A.ag2=new B.ap("Where does most AI bias actually come from?",A.dxm,0,"Models learn from human-produced data. If the data over-represents one group or repeats a stereotype, the model inherits it. Blaming the model misses the source.")
A.e2l=s(["Closed models are always smarter","Convenience and top-end capability vs control, privacy, and long-term cost","Open models are always free with no cost","They are functionally identical"],t.s)
A.afS=new B.ap("What is the main practical tradeoff between using a closed model (e.g. via API) and running an open-weights model yourself?",A.e2l,1,"Closed = easy, cutting-edge, but you rent it and send your data out. Open-weights = more setup and infra, but the model is yours, runs on your hardware, and does not change under you.")
A.eVA=s(["Only for writing docs \u2014 never real code","It replaces engineers entirely, no human review","Inline suggestions, chat pair-programming, and agents that edit files \u2014 with a human reviewing","Just for autocomplete of variable names"],t.s)
A.aeN=new B.ap("Which of these best describes how AI is used in real coding workflows today?",A.eVA,2,"Modern AI coding is a spectrum: autocomplete \u2192 chat \u2192 full multi-file agents. In every serious workflow, a human still reviews the diff \u2014 the AI writes; the developer approves.")
A.bSq=s(["Typing prompts really fast","Memorising every model's name","Owning the biggest GPU","Judgement \u2014 knowing what to ask, what to trust, and when to override"],t.s)
A.agT=new B.ap("As AI keeps improving, what is likely to matter more, not less, for the humans using it?",A.bSq,3,"The models will keep getting better at generating. The durable human skill is judgement \u2014 framing the problem, checking the answer, and knowing when the machine is confidently wrong.")
A.GE=s([A.agO,A.aej,A.agI,A.aeo,A.adZ,A.agm,A.agc,A.afo,A.ael,A.age,A.agg,A.agz,A.af9,A.af_,A.agV,A.afb,A.ag2,A.afS,A.aeN,A.agT],t.Q)
