A.b74=s(["Software that finds patterns in data","A robot that thinks like a human","Any computer program at all","A living conscious mind"],t.s)
A.ae8=new B.ap("Which of these describes AI most accurately today?",A.b74,0,"Modern AI = pattern matching at massive scale. Not sentient, not conscious, not planning \u2014 just really good at 'this is like that'.")
A.f9f=s(["Examples (data)","Emotions","Instincts","Sleep"],t.s)
A.aeR=new B.ap("What does an AI need to learn?",A.f9f,0,"Every AI is trained on examples. No data, nothing to learn.")
A.aOe=s(["Guesses using patterns from examples it has seen","Reasons from first principles like a philosopher","Asks another AI for permission","Randomly picks an answer"],t.s)
A.agS=new B.ap("What does an AI do when it sees something new?",A.aOe,0,"AI predicts based on what looked similar in training. That's why it can be confidently wrong on things outside its data.")
A.b_w=s([A.ae8,A.aeR,A.agS],t.Q)
A.ai3=new B.cq(1,"AI is software that finds patterns in data.","AI isn't magic and it isn't a robot. It's a program that reads lots of examples and learns what patterns those examples share. When you show it something new, it guesses using the patterns it's seen before.",A.b_w,"In one sentence: what surprised you most about how AI actually works?")
A.epv=s(["AI","Machine Learning","Deep Learning","Neural nets"],t.s)
A.af1=new B.ap("Which is the biggest umbrella term?",A.epv,0,"AI > ML > DL. Nested from broad to specific.")
A.aRa=s(["Machine Learning","Statistics only","Robotics","Cryptography"],t.s)
A.agL=new B.ap("Deep Learning is a subset of\u2026",A.aRa,0,"DL is one branch of ML. It uses multi-layered neural nets \u2014 'deep' means many layers.")
A.cwA=s(["Yes \u2014 it fits under the AI umbrella","No, only ML counts","No, only DL counts","Only if it uses a GPU"],t.s)
A.aeJ=new B.ap("Is a hand-written expert system (if/else rules) considered AI?",A.cwA,0,"Traditional rule-based expert systems are still AI. They just aren't machine learning \u2014 the rules are hand-coded, not learned from data.")
A.euR=s([A.af1,A.agL,A.aeJ],t.Q)
A.ahs=new B.cq(2,"ML, DL, AI \u2014 nested boxes, not different things.","AI is the big umbrella term. Machine Learning (ML) is one way to build AI. Deep Learning (DL) is one flavour of ML. Every deep learning system is an AI, but not every AI uses deep learning.",A.euR,"In your own words: what makes ML different from a hand-coded expert system?")
A.f7a=s(["Colder ambient temperatures","Massive amounts of digital data","Cheap parallel GPU compute","Better model architectures (transformers)"],t.s)
A.afg=new B.ap("Which of these is NOT one of the reasons AI took off recently?",A.f7a,0,"Data, compute, and algorithms \u2014 the three-legged stool of modern AI. Temperature has nothing to do with it.")
A.eMj=s(["GPUs","CPUs","ASICs only","Sound cards"],t.s)
A.agd=new B.ap("What kind of chip is most used to train modern AI models?",A.eMj,0,"GPUs were built for graphics but happen to be perfect for the matrix math AI needs. NVIDIA got very lucky (and rich).")
A.dMv=s(["Transformer","Perceptron","Long Short-Term Memory","Support Vector Machine"],t.s)
A.aeh=new B.ap("The model architecture behind most modern LLMs is called\u2026",A.dMv,0,"The transformer, introduced in a 2017 paper called 'Attention Is All You Need', is the architecture behind GPT, Claude, Gemini \u2014 basically every modern LLM.")
A.eBa=s([A.afg,A.agd,A.aeh],t.Q)
A.ahV=new B.cq(3,"Why AI works now (and didn't 20 years ago).","Three things clicked at once: massive data (the internet), cheap parallel compute (GPUs), and better algorithms (the transformer). Any one on its own wasn't enough. All three together = the AI boom.",A.eBa,"Where in your life do you already interact with AI without thinking about it?")
A.b5Y=s([A.ai3,A.ahs,A.ahV],t.I2)
A.aik=new B.fA(1,"Foundations","What AI actually is, in plain English.",A.b5Y)
A.eFd=s(["Classical machine learning","Rule-based expert systems","Generative AI","Regression models"],t.s)
A.af4=new B.ap("Which family produces brand-new content rather than sorting existing content?",A.eFd,2,"Generative models learn a distribution well enough to sample new items from it \u2014 text, pixels, audio, video.")
A.eNw=s(["A hand-coded rulebook","Generative AI","A search engine","Deep learning"],t.s)
A.afl=new B.ap("A face-unlock on your phone is best described as\u2026",A.eNw,3,"Face recognition uses deep neural nets trained on huge image sets. It classifies (you vs not-you), it does not generate.")
A.eoR=s(["It only runs on CPUs","It works without any training data","It never uses labels","It stacks many layers to learn richer patterns"],t.s)
A.ag1=new B.ap("Deep learning is different from classical ML mainly because\u2026",A.eoR,3,"Depth = layers of transformation. Each layer builds on the last, so the network can learn very abstract features.")
A.czw=s([A.af4,A.afl,A.ag1],t.Q)
A.ahZ=new B.cq(1,"AI has three big families: ML, deep learning, generative.","Machine Learning (ML) is the classic family \u2014 feed a program labelled examples and it learns to sort new ones. Deep Learning is a bigger, layered version of ML that shines on messy inputs like images and speech. Generative AI is the newest family \u2014 it produces new text, images, or audio rather than just labelling existing stuff.\n\nFor instance: a spam filter is classic ML. A face-unlock is deep learning. ChatGPT writing a poem is generative.",A.czw,"Which of the three families do you interact with most often \u2014 and which had you never noticed until now?")
A.bm1=s(["Being retrained on your question","Downloading the internet in real time","Running inference \u2014 using its already-trained weights","Learning from you permanently"],t.s)
A.aeY=new B.ap("When you ask an AI chatbot a question, the model is\u2026",A.bm1,2,"Your chat does not change the model. Inference reads the frozen weights. Training happens separately, in advance.")
A.eAr=s(["Training must happen in outer space","Inference secretly uses a different model","Training touches billions of examples and adjusts every weight","Inference costs more, actually"],t.s)
A.agw=new B.ap("Why is training so much more expensive than inference?",A.eAr,2,"Training a frontier model can involve trillions of tokens and tens of thousands of GPUs. Inference is a single pass.")
A.eUS=s(["Stay frozen at whatever it knew when training finished","Automatically update overnight","Slowly forget everything","Learn from user chats"],t.s)
A.agE=new B.ap("A trained model that never sees new data will\u2026",A.eUS,0,"This is why models have a knowledge cutoff. Nothing seeps in after training unless someone retrains or connects tools.")
A.dx5=s([A.aeY,A.agw,A.agE],t.Q)
A.ai_=new B.cq(2,"Training vs inference: two very different modes.","Training is the one-time (or occasional) heavy lift where a model chews through millions of examples and gradually adjusts its internal numbers to get predictions right. It can take days or months and cost real money.\n\nInference is what happens every time you type a prompt: the trained model runs forward once and produces an answer. It is thousands of times cheaper than training. When you use ChatGPT, you are doing inference on a model that was trained months ago.",A.dx5,"If a chatbot does not learn from your conversation, how does that change how much you rely on it for up-to-date facts?")
A.aZe=s(["A conscious general intelligence","A digital human brain","Narrow AI \u2014 good at specific tasks","A form of AGI"],t.s)
A.afd=new B.ap("Every AI system deployed today is best described as\u2026",A.aZe,2,"Even the most general-feeling chatbot is a narrow system trained for text prediction, not a mind.")
A.aOz=s(["Have a fancier logo","Be trained on smaller data","Learn any new task about as fast as a human","Only work on Sundays"],t.s)
A.agk=new B.ap("AGI would be different because it would\u2026",A.aOz,2,"The generality is the whole point \u2014 one system, many domains, minimal retraining.")
A.bf5=s(["They were trained on so much text that many topics overlap","They secretly consult a human","They have real emotions now","They run on quantum hardware"],t.s)
A.ag7=new B.ap("Why do modern chatbots feel more general than earlier AI?",A.bf5,0,"Scale gives the illusion of breadth. Under the hood it is still next-token prediction over patterns it has seen.")
A.ec7=s([A.afd,A.agk,A.ag7],t.Q)
A.ahT=new B.cq(3,"Narrow AI vs AGI: where we actually are.","Narrow AI is very good at one thing at a time \u2014 translation, chess, image tagging, writing emails. It looks impressive because each thing was learned from mountains of data. Every AI you interact with today is narrow, even the flashy ones.\n\nArtificial General Intelligence (AGI) is the hypothetical goal: a system that can pick up any new task the way a human can, without special retraining. Modern chatbots feel general because they can talk about many topics, but they are still pattern matchers dressed in a wide costume.",A.ec7,"What is one task you would trust today's AI with fully, and one you would never let it handle alone?")
A.dSW=s([A.ahZ,A.ai_,A.ahT],t.I2)
A.ain=new B.fA(2,"What Is AI, Really?","The three big families and where they sit on the spectrum.",A.dSW)
A.fa6=s(["The physical weight of the server","A password to the model","The colour of the interface","How much attention each input gets when combined"],t.s)
A.ah6=new B.ap("What do the weights inside a neural network represent?",A.fa6,3,"Weights are the knobs the model learns. Big weight = this input matters. Near-zero weight = ignore it.")
A.cjj=s(["Capable of solving any problem","Illegal without a licence","Very limited \u2014 the power comes from combining many","A conscious agent"],t.s)
A.af5=new B.ap("A single neuron on its own is\u2026",A.cjj,2,"One neuron is basically a weighted sum. Stack thousands in layers and you can approximate stunningly complex patterns.")
A.elo=s(["They speed up the internet","They save disk space","They let each stage build on features found by the previous","They add colour to the output"],t.s)
A.ah4=new B.ap("Layers in a neural network are useful because\u2026",A.elo,2,"Early layers spot edges or letters. Middle layers spot shapes or phrases. Late layers spot concepts.")
A.aJN=s([A.ah6,A.af5,A.ah4],t.Q)
A.ahO=new B.cq(1,"Neurons and layers: the basic building blocks.","A neural network is a stack of very simple units called neurons. Each neuron takes some numbers in, multiplies them by its personal weights, adds a bias, and passes the result on. Nothing dramatic \u2014 the magic comes from stacking millions of them together.\n\nNeurons are grouped into layers. The first layer sees your raw input (pixels, tokens). Each middle layer combines what the previous layer noticed. The final layer produces the output (a class, a next word, a score). Think of it like an assembly line where each station adds a bit more insight.",A.aJN,'When you picture a neural network, what image comes to mind \u2014 and does the "assembly line" version fit or fight it?')
A.emM=s(["What time it is","Which GPU to buy","When to stop being polite","How wrong its current guess was"],t.s)
A.aeO=new B.ap("The loss function during training tells the model\u2026",A.emM,3,"Loss is the signal. Without a way to measure wrong, there is no direction to improve in.")
A.aR9=s(["Descending literal stairs","A special type of chip","Nudging weights in the direction that lowers the loss","Deleting outliers"],t.s)
A.afB=new B.ap("Gradient descent means\u2026",A.aR9,2,"Imagine walking downhill on a foggy mountain \u2014 you cannot see the valley, but you can feel which way is down.")
A.e18=s(["Already an expert","Random \u2014 its predictions are noise until training","Impossible to train","Aware of itself"],t.s)
A.aeP=new B.ap("A newly initialised neural network is essentially\u2026",A.e18,1,"Random weights are the starting point. Training is the long process of turning randomness into structure.")
A.e5o=s([A.aeO,A.afB,A.aeP],t.Q)
A.ahQ=new B.cq(2,"Training = adjusting the knobs until predictions are right.",'A fresh neural network is basically random. You show it an example, it guesses badly, and a scoring function measures how wrong the guess was. That "how wrong" number is called the loss.\n\nThen comes gradient descent: the training loop nudges every weight a tiny bit in the direction that would have made the loss smaller. Repeat billions of times across millions of examples. No single step is impressive; the accumulation is.',A.e5o,"Learning by getting a lot of things a little bit less wrong \u2014 where else in life have you seen that pattern work?")
A.dAC=s(["They use less electricity","They can build features on features, layer by layer","They never make mistakes","They run without data"],t.s)
A.agQ=new B.ap("The main advantage of deep networks over shallow ones is\u2026",A.dAC,1,"Each layer is a new vocabulary built from the previous. Depth gives the model a hierarchy of ideas.")
A.enl=s(["Full objects like cats","Basic edges and colour patches","Sentences","Room temperature"],t.s)
A.agA=new B.ap("In an image-recognition network, early layers usually detect\u2026",A.enl,1,"This is one of the most-studied results in deep learning. Structure emerges from the raw pixels up.")
A.b0Y=s(["It is against the rules","They cost extra","The network learns those intermediate features on its own","They do not exist"],t.s)
A.af7=new B.ap("Nobody hand-codes the middle layers of a deep network because\u2026",A.b0Y,2,"This is the payoff of end-to-end learning: you specify the input and the target, and the middle self-organises.")
A.aML=s([A.agQ,A.agA,A.af7],t.Q)
A.ai4=new B.cq(3,"Why depth works: layered abstraction.",'A shallow network can only mix its inputs in shallow ways. A deep network can build features on top of features on top of features. That layered abstraction is the reason deep learning blew past classical methods on hard problems.\n\nFor instance: in an image net, early layers detect edges and colours, middle layers detect eyes and wheels, late layers detect "cat" or "car". Nobody hand-programmed the middle layers to look for eyes \u2014 the network invented that idea on its own because it was useful for the task.',A.aML,'Where in your own thinking do you notice "layers" \u2014 building a big idea out of smaller ones you already understood?')
A.esn=s([A.ahO,A.ahQ,A.ai4],t.I2)
A.aip=new B.fA(3,"Neural Networks Demystified","Neurons, weights, and why depth matters \u2014 no maths.",A.esn)
A.eKl=s(["A whole paragraph","A single letter, always","A short chunk of text \u2014 often part of a word","A file on disk"],t.s)
A.ae4=new B.ap("A token in a language model is roughly\u2026",A.eKl,2,"On average, one token is around four English characters, though it varies wildly by language and content.")
A.dQm=s(["Words are illegal in some countries","It sounds more technical","Tokens are always cheaper","Tokens are what the model actually processes"],t.s)
A.afL=new B.ap("Why do models charge per token instead of per word?",A.dQm,3,"Compute cost tracks tokens, so pricing tracks tokens. A dense technical prompt costs more than plain chat.")
A.em8=s(["Be a single tidy token","Break into several smaller tokens","Crash the model","Be silently deleted"],t.s)
A.ag3=new B.ap("A very rare or made-up word will typically\u2026",A.em8,1,"The tokenizer only knows the pieces it was trained on. Novel strings get sliced into familiar sub-pieces.")
A.aIV=s([A.ae4,A.afL,A.ag3],t.Q)
A.ahj=new B.cq(1,"Tokens: how AI actually reads your text.",'Language models do not read letters or whole words. They read tokens \u2014 chunks of text that are usually a few characters long. Common words often map to a single token; rare words break into pieces.\n\nFor instance: "hello" is one token. "hellooo" might be three. A URL can be a dozen. Tokens matter because every model has limits and costs measured in tokens, not words. If a reply feels chopped off, the token counter probably filled up.',A.aIV,"If your favourite domain jargon breaks into many tokens, what does that suggest about the cost of chatting about it?")
A.b3d=s(["Physical monitor","Long-term memory of every user","Working memory of tokens for this single call","Internet connection"],t.s)
A.ae0=new B.ap("The context window is the model's\u2026",A.b3d,2,"Think of it as the desk the model is working on. Anything off the desk is invisible for that turn.")
A.esw=s(["It is lying","The internet went down","You paid the wrong tier","Older messages fell out of the context window"],t.s)
A.agn=new B.ap('When a chatbot "forgets" earlier messages in a long chat, it usually means\u2026',A.esw,3,"Most chat apps quietly trim the oldest turns to fit new ones in. There is no persistent memory unless the app adds one.")
A.dKF=s(["It cannot hold documents","It only works offline","It disables the keyboard","It costs more and can dilute the model's focus"],t.s)
A.afW=new B.ap("A downside of a very large context window is\u2026",A.dKF,3,"More tokens = more compute per call, and evidence buried in very long context is easier to miss.")
A.eI6=s([A.ae0,A.agn,A.afW],t.Q)
A.ahw=new B.cq(2,"Context window: what the model can hold in mind.",'The context window is the total number of tokens a model can consider at once \u2014 your prompt, any attached documents, and the reply it is generating. Everything outside that window does not exist to the model.\n\nA small window is like a sticky note. A giant window is like a whole notebook. Bigger is usually better, but bigger also costs more and can dilute attention on the important bits. When a chatbot "forgets" what you said earlier, it usually means the older messages have scrolled out of the window.',A.eI6,"What is one long document you'd love a model to hold entirely in its context \u2014 and what would you ask it about?")
A.dTq=s(["More creative and random","Faster to type","Very repetitive and predictable","Impossible to read"],t.s)
A.agj=new B.ap("A temperature near 0 will make the model's replies\u2026",A.dTq,2,"Low temperature is great for facts and code, bad for brainstorming \u2014 the model picks the safe word every time.")
A.dLq=s(["Higher temperature","Zero context","Temperature of exactly 0","No prompt at all"],t.s)
A.agD=new B.ap("For creative writing you would usually want\u2026",A.dLq,0,"Turn up the randomness and the model surprises you. Turn it down and it plays it safe.")
A.f41=s(["Only ever picking the top word","Ignoring the prompt","Rejecting long words","Sampling from the smallest group of tokens whose probabilities add up past a threshold"],t.s)
A.agv=new B.ap("Top-p (nucleus sampling) works by\u2026",A.f41,3,"Top-p prunes the long tail. It keeps the plausible options and drops the truly weird ones.")
A.eW7=s([A.agj,A.agD,A.agv],t.Q)
A.ahB=new B.cq(3,"Temperature and top-p: the randomness dials.","When a language model picks the next token, it does not just grab the single most likely option. It samples from a probability distribution. Temperature and top-p are the two dials that shape that sampling.\n\nTemperature near 0 makes the model boring and deterministic \u2014 it almost always picks the top choice. Higher temperature makes it more adventurous and creative, sometimes at the cost of accuracy. Top-p limits the sampling to the smallest set of tokens whose combined probability is above a threshold. Low top-p = safe. High top-p = wild.",A.eW7,"Would you rather have an AI that surprises you, or one that is boringly consistent \u2014 and does that depend on the task?")
A.eU8=s([A.ahj,A.ahw,A.ahB],t.I2)
A.aiq=new B.fA(4,"Language Models 101","Tokens, context, and the dials that shape every reply.",A.eU8)
A.eUt=s(["What the user types","A hidden instruction that shapes how the model behaves","The model's own opinion","A shortcut key"],t.s)
A.af8=new B.ap("The system prompt is\u2026",A.eUt,1,"It runs before the first user message. Everything the model does is coloured by it, even if the user never sees it.")
A.b1Y=s(["The user's home address","A random emoji","The colour of the app","Role, audience, and behaviour rules"],t.s)
A.agb=new B.ap("A good system prompt usually specifies\u2026",A.b1Y,3,'The classic recipe: "You are a [role] helping [audience]. Follow these rules. Never do X."')
A.dXq=s(["They run on different planets","They speak different languages under the hood","They have very different system prompts","One is a real human"],t.s)
A.afe=new B.ap("If two chatbots feel wildly different in personality it is often because\u2026",A.dXq,2,"Same base model, different system prompt, totally different vibe. Personality is a prompt, not a soul.")
A.e5a=s([A.af8,A.agb,A.afe],t.Q)
A.ahJ=new B.cq(1,"The system prompt: setting the stage before the show.",'Every serious AI app quietly starts each conversation with a hidden instruction called the system prompt. It tells the model who it is, what it is for, and what it should refuse. The user prompt is what you type on top of that stage.\n\nFor instance: "You are a friendly maths tutor for 10-year-olds. Never solve the problem outright \u2014 ask one guiding question at a time." That framing colours every reply the model gives, without you having to repeat it.',A.e5a,"If you were writing a system prompt for your own personal assistant, what three rules would you insist on?")
A.b0i=s(["Give up and rewrite the entire prompt from scratch","Ask a targeted follow-up in the same thread","Switch to a smaller model","Restart their computer"],t.s)
A.afG=new B.ap("A more experienced AI user, when they get a mediocre reply, will usually\u2026",A.b0i,1,'Follow-ups are free context. You are teaching the model exactly what "better" means for you.')
A.eKr=s(["Adds a constraint or new example","Repeats the exact same request","Is a single vague word","Says nothing"],t.s)
A.ag_=new B.ap("A useful follow-up prompt often does which of these?",A.eKr,0,'Specificity is the whole game. "Make it shorter and aimed at a sceptical CFO" is worth ten "make it better".')
A.dYM=s(["It wastes tokens usefully","It bypasses safety filters","It is a hidden feature","The model reads all the prior turns as context for the next reply"],t.s)
A.afQ=new B.ap("Iterating in the same thread is powerful because\u2026",A.dYM,3,"Every reply is generated with the full transcript in view. Your last critique is guidance for the next attempt.")
A.aSg=s([A.afG,A.ag_,A.afQ],t.Q)
A.ahi=new B.cq(2,"Iteration beats perfection: refine, don't re-type.",'The first answer a chatbot gives is rarely the best answer. The people who get great output are the people who ask follow-ups: "shorter", "more concrete", "rewrite for a sceptical reader", "add a code example". Each turn tightens the reply.\n\nThis is a habit shift. Newcomers throw away a mediocre reply and start a whole new prompt. Regulars stay in the same thread, treating the chat like a conversation with an eager intern who just needs one more nudge.',A.aSg,"What is one prompt you gave up on recently that a single follow-up might have rescued?")
A.eck=s(["You need advanced legal reasoning","You want the smartest answer possible","The task is high-volume and simple, like tagging","You are debugging a rare edge case"],t.s)
A.ah7=new B.ap("A good reason to use a smaller, cheaper model is\u2026",A.eck,2,"At scale, the cost gap dwarfs the quality gap for easy tasks. Save the frontier for hard calls.")
A.e23=s(["A subtle, multi-step reasoning task","Renaming 10,000 rows","A one-word yes/no","Formatting bullet points"],t.s)
A.afO=new B.ap("A good reason to reach for the frontier model is\u2026",A.e23,0,"Complexity, ambiguity, and stakes are the signals. Pay for the brains when you need them.")
A.doc=s(["A branding decision","A fashion contest","A cost-vs-quality tradeoff per task","Fixed forever after the first pick"],t.s)
A.afp=new B.ap("Model choice is essentially\u2026",A.doc,2,"The same person's toolkit might use three or four models on the same day, each for what it is best at.")
A.f_4=s([A.ah7,A.afO,A.afp],t.Q)
A.ahX=new B.cq(3,"Which model, when? Cost vs quality tradeoffs.","Frontier models are smart but slow and expensive. Small models are cheap and fast but shallower. The trick is to match the model to the job: a frontier model for a nuanced legal question, a small model for a bulk translation task.\n\nA useful rule of thumb: if you'd assign this task to a seasoned senior colleague, reach for the frontier model. If you'd hand it to a diligent intern, a smaller model is usually fine \u2014 and ten times cheaper.",A.f_4,"Think of one task you always throw at the biggest model \u2014 could a smaller model handle 80% of it for a tenth of the cost?")
A.eel=s([A.ahJ,A.ahi,A.ahX],t.I2)
A.aim=new B.fA(5,"Working With the Big Chatbots","System prompts, iteration, and picking the right model.",A.eel)
A.eDG=s(["Retrieve facts from a database","Predict plausible text, not verified truth","Consult the internet in real time","Ask a human before answering"],t.s)
A.afF=new B.ap("The root cause of AI hallucination is that models are trained to\u2026",A.eDG,1,"Plausibility, not truth, is the training signal. The model is a fluent guesser with a very wide vocabulary.")
A.cgt=s(["Comes in ALL CAPS","Refuses to end","Appears in a special colour","Sounds confident and well-structured"],t.s)
A.aeg=new B.ap("A hallucinated answer usually\u2026",A.cgt,3,"Hallucinations look like good answers. That is exactly what makes them dangerous \u2014 no red squiggly warning.")
A.etv=s(["Basic arithmetic","A very specific named fact it has never seen","Whether the sky is blue","How to say hello in Spanish"],t.s)
A.aeS=new B.ap("Which kind of question is a language model most likely to hallucinate on?",A.etv,1,"Obscure names, exact dates, rare citations \u2014 these are classic hallucination hotspots. Common patterns are safer.")
A.eAL=s([A.afF,A.aeg,A.aeS],t.Q)
A.ahE=new B.cq(1,"Language models complete text \u2014 they don't verify it.","A language model is trained to predict the most plausible next chunk of text. Plausible is not the same as true. When the model has no real information, it will still produce something that reads correctly \u2014 because sounding right is literally its job.\n\nThat is the root of hallucination: confident, well-formed output that happens to be wrong. It is not lying, it is not guessing to fool you \u2014 it is doing exactly what it was trained to do, on a question where the training didn't cover the true answer.",A.eAL,"Have you ever received a very confident answer from AI that turned out to be wrong? What tipped you off?")
A.e7r=s(['A hedged, "I\'m not certain but\u2026" phrasing','A "study" or citation that you cannot find anywhere',"A short reply","A friendly tone"],t.s)
A.aec=new B.ap("A classic red flag in an AI answer is\u2026",A.e7r,1,"Fabricated citations are one of the best-documented failure modes. Always click the source.")
A.dYf=s(["Increase your trust","End the conversation","Lower your trust, not raise it","Trigger applause"],t.s)
A.ag6=new B.ap("On niche topics, extreme confidence from an AI should\u2026",A.dYf,2,"Real experts qualify their answers. A model that sounds surer than an expert is often less reliable.")
A.cIO=s(["A verification step","To be tattooed on your arm","Immediate use in a slide","A retweet"],t.s)
A.agP=new B.ap('A suspiciously precise number ("74.3% of professionals\u2026") in an AI answer probably deserves\u2026',A.cIO,0,"Precision without a linked source is often invented. The exact decimal is a warning sign, not a badge of quality.")
A.c6X=s([A.aec,A.ag6,A.agP],t.Q)
A.ahh=new B.cq(2,"Red flags: how to spot a shaky AI answer.","A few tells are almost universal. Suspiciously specific numbers with no source. Names of studies or books that don't seem to exist when you search. Extremely round or symmetric statistics. Perfectly quotable quotes from famous people. Legal or medical certainty without caveats.\n\nConfidence is not evidence. In fact, the more polished and certain an answer sounds on a niche topic, the more careful you should be. Real experts hedge; models often don't.",A.c6X,"Which of these red flags have you been ignoring in your own use of AI? Which will you now look out for?")
A.f6u=s(["Every word the model writes","Only the load-bearing claims that matter","Only images","Nothing \u2014 trust the model"],t.s)
A.afy=new B.ap("The 10-second sanity check applies to\u2026",A.f6u,1,"Verify what you'll actually rely on. Skimming the filler is fine; the citation is not.")
A.dDc=s(["The model's tone","The font","The reply length","A specific named fact or citation"],t.s)
A.aga=new B.ap("The best target for a quick verification is usually\u2026",A.dDc,3,"Names, dates, and quoted numbers are cheap to check and expensive to get wrong.")
A.ezx=s(["That the AI is always right","Nothing changes","Which topics the AI is repeatedly wrong on","The colour scheme"],t.s)
A.af2=new B.ap("Verification builds intuition because you start noticing\u2026",A.ezx,2,"After a few catches, you get a feel for where the model is a good witness and where it is a bad one.")
A.e3z=s([A.afy,A.aga,A.af2],t.Q)
A.ahv=new B.cq(3,"The 10-second sanity check.","You don't need to fact-check every sentence \u2014 just the ones that matter. A quick habit: for each load-bearing claim in an AI answer, spend ten seconds. Search the exact phrase. Open the top result. See if the fact holds.\n\nTen seconds beats zero seconds by a mile. It also builds intuition for where the model is reliable (common patterns, reasoning steps, syntax) and where it is not (specific names, dates, citations, live data).",A.e3z,"Pick one AI-produced claim from this week. Spend 10 seconds checking it. What did you find?")
A.cDE=s([A.ahE,A.ahh,A.ahv],t.I2)
A.aii=new B.fA(6,"Hallucinations & Trust","Why AI makes things up and how to catch it in the act.",A.cDE)
A.f2V=s(["Getting you from blank page to rough draft","Deciding what you actually mean to say","Signing off on your reputation","Choosing your friends"],t.s)
A.afK=new B.ap("Where does AI shine most in a writing workflow?",A.f2V,0,"The blank page is the biggest tax on writing. AI is the tax cut. What follows is still your job.")
A.b_8=s(["Publish it verbatim","Edit only the title","Rewrite the parts a real reader will judge you on","Delete the whole thing"],t.s)
A.aeF=new B.ap("A good rule for editing AI drafts is\u2026",A.b_8,2,"Skim the boilerplate; sweat the sections a reader will remember you by.")
A.bv8=s(["They're slightly generic and miss the specific point you meant","They contain zero words","They're too short to read","They cannot render text"],t.s)
A.ah1=new B.ap("AI-generated drafts most often go wrong when\u2026",A.bv8,0,"Genericness is the default failure mode. Your edit is what turns it into your writing.")
A.emX=s([A.afK,A.aeF,A.ah1],t.Q)
A.ahY=new B.cq(1,"First drafts vs final drafts: divide the labour.","AI is very good at getting you from a blank page to a rough draft. It is much less good at deciding what actually needs to be said, or protecting your reputation with the reader. The productive split: let the model generate a scaffold, then you edit like a ruthless colleague.\n\nFor instance: ask for a first-pass FAQ, then rewrite the three questions your customers actually ask. Ask for a first draft cover letter, then rewrite the paragraph about you. The model handles the volume; you handle the taste.",A.emX,"What is one recurring piece of writing you dread \u2014 and could you split it into a rough draft and a final edit tomorrow?")
A.cuc=s(["Describe your voice in adjectives","Show it two or three examples of your actual writing","Whisper","Change your font"],t.s)
A.afV=new B.ap("The fastest way to get an AI to write in your voice is to\u2026",A.cuc,1,'"Warm but concise" is guesswork. Three real examples are gold. Show, don\'t tell.')
A.aJ5=s(["Aggressively edgy","Silent","Illiterate","Friendly and mildly corporate"],t.s)
A.aeZ=new B.ap("The default voice of most chatbots is best described as\u2026",A.aJ5,3,"Trained on the internet, tuned for safety and agreeableness. That's the flat middle you're fighting.")
A.f25=s(["Ask for a piece it hasn't seen and check if it sounds like you","Ask it to repeat one of your examples verbatim","Check the model name","Ask if it likes you"],t.s)
A.aeK=new B.ap("A quick test that the model has learned your voice is\u2026",A.f25,0,"Novel task, familiar voice = the pattern has taken. If it only mimics on the training examples, dig deeper.")
A.ecc=s([A.afV,A.aeZ,A.aeK],t.Q)
A.ahK=new B.cq(2,"Voice and tone: teach the model to sound like you.","Out of the box, chatbots write in a friendly, corporate voice \u2014 a bit like a hotel welcome pamphlet. If you want your voice, you have to show it your voice. Paste two or three examples of your actual writing and ask the model to match tone, sentence length, and vocabulary.\n\nFor instance: \"Here are three emails I've sent. Write a fourth in the same voice.\" The model is very good at pattern-matching style once it has a small sample to copy. This is show-don't-tell.",A.ecc,"What three examples of your writing best represent how you actually sound? Could you paste them into a prompt tomorrow?")
A.efA=s(["Deciding what to say to your board","Choosing your career path","Choosing whether to have children","Turning a wall of text into a clean bullet list"],t.s)
A.afT=new B.ap('Which of these is a "utility" writing win for AI?',A.efA,3,"Reformatting is nearly perfect for AI: mechanical, reversible, no judgement required.")
A.cyU=s(["The correct answer is usually clear and low-stakes","It requires deep judgement","It has to be creative","Only humans can spell"],t.s)
A.aeW=new B.ap("A big reason grammar/spelling clean-up is a good AI task is\u2026",A.cyU,0,"Low ambiguity, high volume, easy to verify. Basically the perfect AI cocktail.")
A.cAi=s(["Compounding time saved across many small tasks","A brand new writing style","Fewer emails to send","A promotion"],t.s)
A.ago=new B.ap("The biggest gain from utility writing tasks is\u2026",A.cAi,0,"One email saved doesn't matter. Fifty emails a week saved does. Utility wins compound quietly.")
A.c8i=s([A.afT,A.aeW,A.ago],t.Q)
A.ahG=new B.cq(3,"The utility win: grammar, spelling, and structure.","Beyond glamour tasks, AI quietly kills the boring parts of writing. Grammar clean-up. Spelling. Reordering paragraphs so the strongest point leads. Turning a wall of text into a bullet list. Turning a bullet list into a paragraph.\n\nThis is the least sexy win and probably the biggest. The ten minutes you used to spend polishing an email or a slide now cost thirty seconds. Do that fifty times a week and it adds up to a full working day.",A.c8i,"What is one boring writing task you do every week that you could hand off entirely to AI starting today?")
A.dA7=s([A.ahY,A.ahK,A.ahG],t.I2)
A.ai9=new B.fA(7,"AI-Powered Writing","Drafts, voice, and the boring bits you never have to touch again.",A.dA7)
A.eQv=s(["Asks the model questions rather than issuing commands","Whispers","Never types","Uses only one word"],t.s)
A.agK=new B.ap("The Socratic style of prompting means the user mostly\u2026",A.eQv,0,"Questions pull reasoning out of the model. Commands just get you finished text you may not understand.")
A.ck_=s(['"Give me the summary."','"Quiz me on this and correct my answers."','"Write a poem."','"Say hi."'],t.s)
A.ae7=new B.ap("To understand a topic more deeply, a better prompt is\u2026",A.ck_,1,"Active recall beats passive reading. Turn the chatbot into a patient examiner.")
A.et5=s(["It flatters the model","It forces the model to look for gaps in your framing","It costs less","It makes replies shorter"],t.s)
A.afv=new B.ap('Asking "what am I missing?" is powerful because\u2026',A.et5,1,"It flips the model from answer-machine to devil's-advocate. Very good for decisions.")
A.eD9=s([A.agK,A.ae7,A.afv],t.Q)
A.ahu=new B.cq(1,"Ask questions, not commands \u2014 the Socratic move.",'Newcomers tell the model what to do. Power users ask the model questions. "Explain this to me like I\'ve never seen it." "What am I missing?" "What\'s the strongest counter-argument?" Questions surface reasoning; commands surface output.\n\nThis is especially powerful when you\'re trying to understand a topic. Instead of "write me a summary of quantum computing", try "quiz me on quantum computing basics, one question at a time, and correct my answers". The second prompt gets you learning; the first gets you a passive read.',A.eD9,"Pick a topic you want to learn. What is one Socratic question you could ask an AI about it right now?")
A.c7Q=s(["Nothing \u2014 the model knows best","Audience, length, and what to keep","The exact font","A random number"],t.s)
A.aff=new B.ap("A good summarisation prompt usually specifies\u2026",A.c7Q,1,"Summary length and audience are the two biggest quality levers. Set both explicitly.")
A.ew4=s(["Being too accurate","Being too short to read","Losing the specific numbers or names that mattered","Refusing to run"],t.s)
A.agq=new B.ap("A common failure mode of AI summaries is\u2026",A.ew4,2,"Models flatten specifics into generalities by default. Ask explicitly for the parts you cannot lose.")
A.bNm=s(["Summarise section by section, then summarise the summaries","Give up","Print the document","Type in ALL CAPS"],t.s)
A.afj=new B.ap("A useful trick for very long documents is to\u2026",A.bNm,0,"This is called hierarchical summarisation. It preserves detail that a one-shot summary would flatten.")
A.ewO=s([A.aff,A.agq,A.afj],t.Q)
A.ahF=new B.cq(2,"Summarising: TL;DR done right.",'The obvious use of AI is "summarise this". The subtle use is telling the model who the summary is for and what it should preserve. A five-bullet summary for a busy executive is a different animal from a two-paragraph brief for a domain expert.\n\nA great summary prompt names the audience, the length, and the "keep or drop" priorities. For instance: "Summarise this article in five bullets for someone who has never read about the topic. Keep specific numbers; drop the quotes."',A.ewO,"What is one document sitting in your inbox that a proper, audience-aware summary would save you an hour on?")
A.dL9=s(["Explain, quiz, review the gaps","Only reading passively","Copy-pasting the answer","Skipping the quiz"],t.s)
A.agW=new B.ap("A powerful studying loop with AI includes\u2026",A.dL9,0,"Active recall + spaced review + a patient partner is a proven learning formula. AI just makes it always-on.")
A.e0N=s(["It compresses your file","The model likes it","You get free credits","You expose your understanding \u2014 the model can point at what's missing"],t.s)
A.ae9=new B.ap("Explaining a concept back to the AI in your own words is powerful because\u2026",A.e0N,3,"This is called the Feynman technique. If you can't explain it simply, you don't really know it yet.")
A.dg2=s(["Ignore them","Feed them back later as a targeted mini-quiz","Print and frame them","Delete the chat"],t.s)
A.aek=new B.ap("A good use of the questions you got wrong is to\u2026",A.dg2,1,"Wrong answers are the map to what you don't know. Come back to them a day or two later.")
A.b_I=s([A.agW,A.ae9,A.aek],t.Q)
A.ahm=new B.cq(3,"Studying with AI: explanations, then quizzes, then gaps.","A model makes an excellent private tutor if you drive the loop: ask for an explanation, ask a follow-up on any part that felt fuzzy, then ask to be quizzed. Track the questions you get wrong \u2014 those are your gaps.\n\nThe pattern that beats passive reading every time: read once \u2192 explain back to the AI in your own words \u2192 let it grade you \u2192 review whatever it flagged. That loop trades a little pride for a lot of retention.",A.b_I,'What is one topic you\'ve been "meaning to learn" \u2014 and could you run one explain-quiz-review loop on it today?')
A.d6b=s([A.ahu,A.ahF,A.ahm],t.I2)
A.aid=new B.fA(8,"AI for Research & Learning","Ask better questions and learn faster with a patient tutor.",A.d6b)
A.evP=s(["Extracting decisions, actions, and owners","Deleting the meeting","Rewriting the transcript in verse","Emailing the whole company"],t.s)
A.aeD=new B.ap("A great AI task for a meeting transcript is\u2026",A.evP,0,"Meetings are dense unstructured audio. Structured output is exactly what AI is built to produce from that.")
A.d1h=s(["Just the recipient's name","The zodiac sign","The user's home address","Tone, length, and the outcome you want"],t.s)
A.aeT=new B.ap("A useful email prompt often specifies\u2026",A.d1h,3,"Same three levers as any prompt \u2014 audience, length, intent. Emails are just short prompts in disguise.")
A.aSC=s(["You get a bonus","They repeat many times a day, every day","They save the company money instantly","They are televised"],t.s)
A.aeq=new B.ap("The reason these small wins add up is\u2026",A.aSC,1,"Compounding is the secret. Fifty tiny wins per week is a workflow that eats a job description alive.")
A.f4m=s([A.aeD,A.aeT,A.aeq],t.Q)
A.ah9=new B.cq(1,"Email and meetings: the quiet productivity wins.",'Nobody brags about it, but the most common AI use at work is right here: drafting the tricky reply, summarising a meeting transcript, extracting the three actions someone buried in a rambling voice note. Small, boring, huge time saver.\n\nFor instance: paste a meeting transcript, ask for a five-bullet summary plus a list of decisions and owners. Or paste an email you don\'t want to write and ask for a first-draft reply "polite, firm, keep it under 120 words". The gains are quiet and daily.',A.f4m,"What is one email or meeting task you procrastinate on \u2014 and could you hand its first draft to AI tomorrow?")
A.f4S=s(["AI now enters your data for you automatically","Colouring cells","Deleting the file","Describing formulas in plain English and getting real syntax back"],t.s)
A.ags=new B.ap("The main productivity win in spreadsheets with AI is\u2026",A.f4S,3,"You bring the intent; AI brings the syntax. The literacy barrier drops from months to minutes.")
A.eZq=s(["Framing it on the wall","Checking it against a known test case","Sharing it publicly","Emailing your manager"],t.s)
A.aeI=new B.ap("When AI writes a formula for you, the crucial step you must not skip is\u2026",A.eZq,1,"The formula usually looks right and sometimes is wrong. Try it on a row where you already know the answer.")
A.cjv=s(["SQL, pivots, and macros","Coffee machines","Printer paper","Nothing else"],t.s)
A.ah0=new B.ap("The same trick that works on Excel formulas also works on\u2026",A.cjv,0,"Anywhere structured syntax hides a simple intent, AI can translate for you. That covers most office tooling.")
A.e6X=s([A.ags,A.aeI,A.ah0],t.Q)
A.ahL=new B.cq(2,"Spreadsheets: describe the formula in plain English.",'The single biggest office skill AI hands you for free is the ability to describe what you want a spreadsheet to do \u2014 in words \u2014 and get the formula. "Sum column B where column A is this month" turns into a real SUMIFS in seconds.\n\nThis is a bigger deal than it sounds. Spreadsheets were gate-kept by formula literacy for forty years. Now the gate is basically open. Same trick works for pivot tables, SQL, Google Sheets scripts, and Excel macros. Describe the shape you want; paste and check the result.',A.e6X,"Is there a spreadsheet task you dread every week because the formula is fiddly? What would you ask AI for first?")
A.crJ=s(["Designing every pixel","Turning a blank deck into a structured outline fast","Buying the software","Speaking on stage for you"],t.s)
A.agF=new B.ap("The biggest AI win in presentation-building is\u2026",A.crJ,1,"Structure first, sparkle later. The outline unblocks you; the design is still yours.")
A.bZE=s(["Audience, goal, and rough length","Your salary","A song lyric","A photo of the office"],t.s)
A.ah3=new B.ap("A good outline prompt includes\u2026",A.bZE,0,"Every outline is a compromise between the speaker, the audience, and the time budget. Name all three.")
A.ek5=s(["Editing structure is cheaper than rewriting prose","Outlines are legally required","Prose is slower to type","Documents are heavier"],t.s)
A.agR=new B.ap("For long documents, iterating on the outline first is faster because\u2026",A.ek5,0,"Fix the argument at the outline stage. The prose writes itself once the skeleton is right.")
A.eMS=s([A.agF,A.ah3,A.agR],t.Q)
A.ai5=new B.cq(3,"Presentations and docs: outline faster, polish later.",'The blank slide is worse than the blank page. AI is very good at going from "I need a 10-slide deck on X for Y audience" to a rough outline with headings, key points, and even suggested chart types. You still design; you just skip the "where do I start" tax.\n\nFor long documents the pattern is the same: brief the model on audience and intent, ask for an outline, react to the outline, then expand section by section. Iterating on a skeleton is faster than sculpting a wall of text.',A.eMS,"What is one deck or document you keep putting off? Could you have an AI-drafted outline in your hands in ten minutes?")
A.egX=s([A.ah9,A.ahL,A.ai5],t.I2)
A.aie=new B.fA(9,"AI in Your Job","Email, spreadsheets, and slides \u2014 the quiet productivity wins.",A.egX)
A.c_C=s(["Clearer context and a defined output format","Adding polite phrases like please and thank you","Using ALL CAPS for emphasis","Writing it in Latin"],t.s)
A.ag5=new B.ap("What makes a good prompt more effective than a vague one?",A.c_C,0,"Models respond to clarity, not manners. Structure the ask, state the audience, and describe the output you want.")
A.dOf=s(["It gives the model information it would otherwise have to guess","It makes the prompt longer so the model works harder","It uses up the free tier faster","It is required by the model"],t.s)
A.agr=new B.ap("What is the primary reason to include context in a prompt?",A.dOf,0,"Context lets the model condition on your situation. Without it, you get a generic answer written for a generic reader.")
A.e0B=s(["Usually makes the response easier to parse and use","Confuses the model","Is always ignored","Requires special permission"],t.s)
A.agi=new B.ap("A prompt that specifies an output format (e.g. JSON, bullet list)\u2026",A.e0B,0,"Format constraints turn the model into a reliable component in a larger system, not just a chat.")
A.dFt=s([A.ag5,A.agr,A.agi],t.Q)
A.ahn=new B.cq(1,"Prompting is packaging: what the model sees is what you get.","A prompt is not a magic incantation. It is a package of context the model reads before it starts writing. Better packaging (clear instructions, relevant background, a defined output format) means better answers. There is no secret keyword \u2014 clarity beats cleverness.",A.dFt,"Think of a prompt you sent recently that gave a mediocre answer. What extra context would have helped the model?")
A.ccR=s(["Providing a few input-output examples inside the prompt","Sending the prompt a few times in a row","Firing several prompts at once","Using a small model"],t.s)
A.afk=new B.ap("Few-shot prompting means\u2026",A.ccR,0,"The examples act as an implicit specification. Cheaper and faster than fine-tuning for many everyday tasks.")
A.d5A=s(["When the style or format is hard to describe in words","When you want a completely random response","Only when using open-source models","Only for image generation"],t.s)
A.afU=new B.ap("When is few-shot most useful?",A.d5A,0,"'Show, don't tell' \u2014 writing style, tone, and edge-case handling are often easier to demonstrate than describe.")
A.eX3=s(["The model over-fits to the examples and copies them too literally","It always requires internet access","It removes your privacy","It only works on Sundays"],t.s)
A.aes=new B.ap("A pitfall of few-shot prompting is\u2026",A.eX3,0,"Vary the examples enough that the pattern generalises. Otherwise the model produces near-clones.")
A.dpW=s([A.afk,A.afU,A.aes],t.Q)
A.ahW=new B.cq(2,"Few-shot prompting: show examples of what you want.","Instead of describing the task, show two or three examples of the input plus the ideal output. The model spots the pattern and follows it. It is often the fastest way to lift quality on a specific style, format, or judgement task.",A.dpW,"Where in your work would showing two or three examples of a good output be easier than describing the rules?")
A.esi=s(["Write out its reasoning before the final answer","Only respond in single-word answers","Skip verification steps","Speak in rhyme"],t.s)
A.agB=new B.ap("Chain-of-thought prompting encourages the model to\u2026",A.esi,0,"Intermediate reasoning acts like scratch paper. The model 'thinks out loud' and reaches better conclusions.")
A.d25=s(["A multi-step maths word problem","A one-word translation","A yes/no question","Reading a colour code"],t.s)
A.afE=new B.ap("Which task benefits MOST from chain-of-thought?",A.d25,0,"The bigger the reasoning gap between question and answer, the more chain-of-thought helps.")
A.cnK=s(["Longer, slower, more expensive responses","It makes the model illegal","It disables images","It resets your account"],t.s)
A.aem=new B.ap("A downside of chain-of-thought is\u2026",A.cnK,0,"More reasoning means more tokens generated \u2014 every extra token is time and cost.")
A.dRn=s([A.agB,A.afE,A.aem],t.Q)
A.ahz=new B.cq(3,"Chain-of-thought: ask the model to reason before answering.","Adding a phrase like 'think step by step' or 'reason through this carefully' tells the model to produce intermediate reasoning before it commits to a final answer. On many tasks \u2014 maths, logic, multi-step decisions \u2014 this measurably improves accuracy.",A.dRn,"When was the last time you asked an AI a question that had multiple steps hidden inside it?")
A.eBf=s([A.ahn,A.ahW,A.ahz],t.I2)
A.aig=new B.fA(10,"Prompt Engineering","Context, examples, and chain-of-thought \u2014 what actually works.",A.eBf)
A.eKB=s(["A retrieval step that fetches relevant documents first","A random-number generator","A better GPU","A translation layer"],t.s)
A.afI=new B.ap("RAG combines a language model with\u2026",A.eKB,0,"The retrieved documents become extra context in the prompt. The model reads them and answers with those facts in hand.")
A.dJn=s(["It lets the model answer using company-specific documents","It removes the need for any model at all","It is free","It runs entirely offline by default"],t.s)
A.aer=new B.ap("Why is RAG popular in enterprise?",A.dJn,0,"You cannot fine-tune a model on every new PDF. RAG lets the model reach data it was never trained on.")
A.dbO=s(["A vector search over embeddings of your documents","A very fast printer","A quantum computer","A random walk"],t.s)
A.ah5=new B.ap("A retriever in a RAG system is typically\u2026",A.dbO,0,"Documents are chunked, embedded into vectors, and stored. At query time, the system finds nearest-neighbour chunks to the question.")
A.aQ9=s([A.afI,A.aer,A.ah5],t.Q)
A.ahq=new B.cq(1,"RAG = search + generation.","Retrieval-Augmented Generation is a pattern where the system first searches a knowledge source (documents, a database, the web) for relevant information, then passes those snippets into the prompt so the model can generate its answer from that grounded context.",A.aQ9,"What documents in your work would you want an AI to be able to reference before answering a question?")
A.b8H=s(["Frequently changing product documentation","Fixed writing style","Simple grammar correction","Choosing an emoji"],t.s)
A.aeC=new B.ap("Which of these favours RAG over fine-tuning?",A.b8H,0,"Retrain nightly? Painful. Re-index nightly? Cheap. Choose RAG when the facts move under you.")
A.aOY=s(["Keeping the vector index fresh and correct","Buying more monitors","Learning a new programming language","Renaming variables"],t.s)
A.aev=new B.ap("A big cost of RAG at scale is\u2026",A.aOY,0,"Stale or messy chunks poison the retrieval step. Data hygiene is where most RAG projects live or die.")
A.egB=s(["The retrieved documents are known before generation","The model has a photographic memory","It runs in a browser","The internet is off"],t.s)
A.agG=new B.ap("RAG lets you cite sources because\u2026",A.egB,0,"You know exactly which chunks you passed in. You can return them as citations alongside the answer.")
A.c0o=s([A.aeC,A.aev,A.agG],t.Q)
A.aho=new B.cq(2,"When RAG wins vs when fine-tuning wins.","RAG shines when facts change often, when data must stay in your own store, or when citation matters. Fine-tuning shines when you need the model to learn a stable style, format, or judgement pattern. Many production systems use both.",A.c0o,"Would you rather trust an answer with a citation you can click, or one without? Why does that matter for your work?")
A.dTd=s(["Combining vector search with keyword search","Using two GPUs","Two humans doing the search","Running the query twice"],t.s)
A.aey=new B.ap("Hybrid retrieval means\u2026",A.dTd,0,"Each retriever covers what the other misses. Merging them raises recall without hurting precision.")
A.aP6=s(["Sort candidate documents by their relevance to the query","Change the model temperature","Overwrite the prompt","Reset your session"],t.s)
A.aeU=new B.ap("A re-ranker in RAG is used to\u2026",A.aP6,0,"Re-rankers are usually smaller specialised models \u2014 cheap to run per candidate, hugely improve quality.")
A.cyj=s(["Retrieving the wrong chunks and confidently answering from them","Retrieval always finding the right answer","The model refusing to write anything","The vector store deleting itself"],t.s)
A.agZ=new B.ap("A common failure mode of a RAG pipeline is\u2026",A.cyj,0,"Garbage in, confident garbage out. Evaluating the retriever is as important as evaluating the model.")
A.coy=s([A.aey,A.aeU,A.agZ],t.Q)
A.ahr=new B.cq(3,"Hybrid retrieval and re-ranking.","Pure vector search misses exact-keyword matches, and pure keyword search misses paraphrase. Hybrid retrieval runs both and merges results. A re-ranker then scores the candidates against the query to pick the top handful for the model.",A.coy,"If an AI cited a source that was clearly the wrong document, how would you handle that in a real product?")
A.e9G=s([A.ahq,A.aho,A.ahr],t.I2)
A.aif=new B.fA(11,"Retrieval-Augmented Generation","Teaching a model to look things up before answering.",A.e9G)
A.eGz=s(["It can call tools and iterate on the results","It has a name","It costs more","It has a mobile app"],t.s)
A.afc=new B.ap('What makes an AI system an "agent" rather than a chatbot?',A.eGz,0,"Tools + a decision loop = agency. Without those, the model is just a very smart text completion.")
A.b8W=s(["A function the model can call, with a defined input and output","A physical wrench","A different AI vendor","A user"],t.s)
A.agt=new B.ap('A "tool" in agent terminology is typically\u2026',A.b8W,0,"Tools are functions \u2014 search, database lookup, sending an email. The model chooses which to call and with what args.")
A.dFZ=s(["It can take actions with real-world consequences before you review them","It costs less","It stops working entirely","It becomes friendlier"],t.s)
A.aeV=new B.ap("The main risk of giving an agent tools is\u2026",A.dFZ,0,"The moment an agent can send email, spend money, or delete files, you need guardrails: confirmations, allowlists, and audit logs.")
A.ckH=s([A.afc,A.agt,A.aeV],t.Q)
A.ahf=new B.cq(1,"An agent is a model plus tools plus a loop.","A chatbot answers one message at a time. An agent decides what to do next, calls a tool (search, calculator, API), reads the result, and decides again. The loop is what makes it an agent \u2014 not just cleverer prompting.",A.ckH,"What is one boring, repeatable task in your work that would be safe to hand to an agent \u2014 and which would you never trust one with?")
A.eLd=s(["Reasoning traces with tool-calling actions","Two models arguing","A React web app","Reversed acting"],t.s)
A.aeG=new B.ap("ReAct is a pattern that combines\u2026",A.eLd,0,"The interleaved thought / action / observation trace is what lets you debug what the agent did and why.")
A.eYd=s(["To review results so far and adjust the plan if needed","To pause for lunch","To lower their price","To upload logs"],t.s)
A.afD=new B.ap('Why do agents often "reflect" partway through a task?',A.eYd,0,"Reflection is checking work mid-flight. Catches mistakes earlier than a single-shot answer would.")
A.bGv=s(["They can drift off-task or loop on the same mistake","They finish instantly","They cost nothing","They cannot use tools"],t.s)
A.afJ=new B.ap("A weakness of long-running agents is\u2026",A.bGv,0,"Loops, off-track exploration, and runaway costs are all real. Budget caps and step limits are standard safeguards.")
A.e0e=s([A.aeG,A.afD,A.afJ],t.Q)
A.ahA=new B.cq(2,"Planning: the agent decides the next step.","A common pattern is ReAct: the model alternates between 'Thought:' and 'Action:' outputs. It reasons about what to do, runs the action, reads the observation, and reasons again. Similar patterns exist under names like plan-and-solve, tree search, and reflection.",A.e0e,"If you had to review an AI agent's reasoning trace, what would be the top thing you would look for?")
A.dF3=s(["A person reviews or approves key steps","No human ever intervenes","The AI has a physical body","The AI is required to lie"],t.s)
A.ag4=new B.ap('A "human-in-the-loop" system is one where\u2026',A.dF3,0,"Approvals, confirmations, and reviews are the seatbelt of an agent system. Cheap to add, expensive to skip.")
A.f1o=s(["One that spends money, sends a message, or deletes data","Reading a public web page","Adding numbers","Changing its own font"],t.s)
A.afX=new B.ap("The best action for an agent to require confirmation on is\u2026",A.f1o,0,"Rule of thumb: irreversible action = pause and confirm.")
A.eV0=s(["Small, scoped agents that automate one process end-to-end","One giant agent that does every job in the company","No agents at all","A different AI for every user"],t.s)
A.ah8=new B.ap("The most useful agent design pattern in most companies today is\u2026",A.eV0,0,"Small scope, clear tools, obvious approval points. Not science fiction \u2014 just very well-drawn boundaries.")
A.beN=s([A.ag4,A.afX,A.ah8],t.Q)
A.ahS=new B.cq(3,"Human-in-the-loop is not going away.",'Even a highly capable agent benefits from human checkpoints on irreversible or high-stakes actions. The goal is not "no humans" \u2014 it is "humans on the interesting decisions, agents on the grinding ones."',A.beN,"Where in your workflow would you WANT AI to pause and check in with you before continuing?")
A.eYz=s([A.ahf,A.ahA,A.ahS],t.I2)
A.ai8=new B.fA(12,"AI Agents","From chatbots to systems that can take actions.",A.eYz)
A.dNx=s(["Can accept and reason over multiple input types","Runs on multiple GPUs","Speaks multiple languages","Uses multiple mice"],t.s)
A.af3=new B.ap("A multi-modal model is one that\u2026",A.dNx,0,"Text plus vision, text plus audio, text plus code \u2014 the mix depends on the model.")
A.cg0=s(["Turns the image into embeddings and processes it alongside the text","Prints the image","Uploads it to a physical server","Ignores it"],t.s)
A.aew=new B.ap("To reason over an image, a modern model typically\u2026",A.cg0,0,"The vision encoder produces vectors. From the model's point of view, the image is 'just more tokens'.")
A.cNX=s(["Reading receipts, forms, and screenshots","Repainting your house","Playing music","Broadcasting radio"],t.s)
A.aex=new B.ap("A common use for vision-language models is\u2026",A.cNX,0,"Document AI \u2014 invoices, IDs, screenshots, whiteboards \u2014 is one of the biggest early wins for multi-modal models.")
A.eci=s([A.af3,A.aew,A.aex],t.Q)
A.aha=new B.cq(1,"Multi-modal models read more than text.","Modern flagship models accept images, audio, and sometimes video alongside text. Internally the model converts each modality into tokens (or embeddings) it can reason about the same way it reasons about words.",A.eci,"What paper document in your life would be easier to deal with if you could just photograph it and ask questions?")
A.cN2=s(["Automatic Speech Recognition","Assisted Sound Repair","Advanced Screen Rendering","Applied Semantic Retrieval"],t.s)
A.aeb=new B.ap("ASR stands for\u2026",A.cN2,0,"ASR turns audio into text. TTS goes the other way.")
A.dcS=s(["Handling accents, background noise, and overlapping speakers","The colour of the microphone","Word count","Font size"],t.s)
A.agy=new B.ap("A frequent challenge for speech models is\u2026",A.dcS,0,"Clean studio speech is easy. Real-world audio \u2014 cafes, phones, kids in the background \u2014 is the hard part.")
A.dLk=s(["They are generated by neural models trained on human recordings","They record every possible word","They use compression only","They call a human on demand"],t.s)
A.ae1=new B.ap("Modern TTS voices sound realistic because\u2026",A.dLk,0,"Neural TTS predicts audio waveforms directly. That's why the pauses, breaths, and intonation feel human.")
A.aTq=s([A.aeb,A.agy,A.ae1],t.Q)
A.ahk=new B.cq(2,"Audio: speech-to-text and text-to-speech.","Speech recognition (ASR) turns audio into text; text-to-speech (TTS) does the reverse. Modern models can do both directions well in dozens of languages and voices, and some can now hold a real-time voice conversation.",A.aTq,"Where would voice-first AI be more useful to you than typing?")
A.ck0=s(["Frames must stay consistent over time","It uses more colours","It requires a licence","It runs backwards"],t.s)
A.aet=new B.ap("Why is video generation harder than image generation?",A.ck0,0,"Temporal coherence \u2014 hands don't randomly turn into paws between frames \u2014 is the whole game.")
A.aX_=s(["Programming language syntax and repository structure","Interior design","Only English","How to run a spreadsheet"],t.s)
A.afr=new B.ap("Modern code models improve on plain text models by learning\u2026",A.aX_,0,"Coding models are trained on huge amounts of open-source code. That structure is what lets them autocomplete a function you never wrote.")
A.eD8=s(["Summarising a long meeting recording","Predicting the weather","Timing your commute","Steering a bicycle"],t.s)
A.aeB=new B.ap("A practical use of a video-understanding model is\u2026",A.eD8,0,"Meeting summarisation, sports highlights, security review \u2014 video AI shines when you have too much footage to watch.")
A.ewn=s([A.aet,A.afr,A.aeB],t.Q)
A.ahP=new B.cq(3,"Video and code as modalities.","Video is essentially images across time \u2014 much heavier to process but the same core idea. Code is text, but with extra structure a model can learn: syntax, imports, types, and how files link together.",A.ewn,"If AI could analyse one video for you and tell you the key moments, which video would you pick?")
A.d5X=s([A.aha,A.ahk,A.ahP],t.I2)
A.ail=new B.fA(13,"Multi-modal AI","Vision, audio, video, code \u2014 one model, many inputs.",A.d5X)
A.eLo=s(["Weights, based on new training examples","Skin colour","Country of origin","Serial number"],t.s)
A.agu=new B.ap("Fine-tuning changes the model's\u2026",A.eLo,0,"Fine-tuning is real training, just on a smaller, targeted dataset built for your task.")
A.dq1=s(["Try harder prompts and add examples before fine-tuning","Buy a bigger GPU","Hire more people","Change the model provider"],t.s)
A.agM=new B.ap("A quick-and-cheap first move is usually to\u2026",A.dq1,0,'Most "we need to fine-tune" projects are secretly "we need better prompts and evals" projects.')
A.egI=s(["Prompt engineering has plateaued and the task has thousands of examples","You have one example","The model hallucinates once","You want the model to be nicer"],t.s)
A.afC=new B.ap("A signal that fine-tuning WILL help is\u2026",A.egI,0,"Fine-tuning wants data volume, a stable target behaviour, and a clear eval you can rerun.")
A.eTS=s([A.agu,A.agM,A.afC],t.Q)
A.ahg=new B.cq(1,"Prompting is cheap. Fine-tuning is durable.","Prompting changes what the model sees at inference time. Fine-tuning changes the model itself \u2014 its weights \u2014 so it behaves a certain way even without special instructions. Fine-tuning is more work, but the result is baked in.",A.eTS,"Have you ever wished a model behaved consistently on a task that prompting alone kept missing? What was it?")
A.eZo=s(["Fine-tuning a small subset of parameters instead of the whole model","A new type of GPU","Compressing images","Speeding up your Wi-Fi"],t.s)
A.ag0=new B.ap("LoRA is a technique for\u2026",A.eZo,0,"LoRA trains tiny adapter matrices. Small storage, small compute, big practical impact.")
A.b78=s(["You can swap adapters to switch behaviours without a full retrain","It removes the base model","It is required for every use","It only works with graphics"],t.s)
A.aft=new B.ap("A benefit of LoRA is that\u2026",A.b78,0,"One base model plus a library of task-specific adapters = one deployment, many specialised behaviours.")
A.aLv=s(["It is much cheaper than full fine-tuning","It teaches the model to write poetry","It requires no data","It works only offline"],t.s)
A.ag9=new B.ap("Parameter-efficient fine-tuning is popular because\u2026",A.aLv,0,"Cost matters. LoRA and friends collapse fine-tuning from thousands of GPU-hours to a handful.")
A.dLL=s([A.ag0,A.aft,A.ag9],t.Q)
A.ahR=new B.cq(2,"LoRA and parameter-efficient fine-tuning.","Full fine-tuning updates every weight, which is expensive. LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) and related methods only train a small set of extra parameters that plug into the base model. You get most of the benefit at a fraction of the cost.",A.dLL,'If you could keep several "flavours" of the same model \u2014 one for customer support, one for internal docs, one for creative writing \u2014 how would that change your team?')
A.f7_=s(["Defines persistent behaviour and constraints for the whole session","Is written in a bigger font","Only applies once","Is optional and ignored"],t.s)
A.aea=new B.ap("A system prompt is different from a user prompt because it\u2026",A.f7_,0,'System prompts are the "always-on" instructions. Set personality, guardrails, formatting there, not per turn.')
A.bBG=s(["Prompt engineering, then RAG, then fine-tuning","Fine-tuning first, prompts last","A new office chair","Change your favourite model each week"],t.s)
A.ah2=new B.ap("A good order of investment for most teams is\u2026",A.bBG,0,"The cheapest changes tend to give the biggest early wins. Only pull the heavy lever once the light ones stop working.")
A.czL=s(["Without evals, you cannot tell if a change helped or hurt","Evaluations are legally required","It doubles the model speed","It is a marketing gimmick"],t.s)
A.aeX=new B.ap("Evaluation matters at every step because\u2026",A.czL,0,"A repeatable eval \u2014 a set of inputs with expected outputs \u2014 is what turns AI work from vibes into engineering.")
A.ben=s([A.aea,A.ah2,A.aeX],t.Q)
A.ai1=new B.cq(3,"The middle ground: RAG plus system prompts.","Before fine-tuning, most teams get very far with two moves: strong system prompts that define the persona and rules, and RAG to give the model the right context per query. Only after that do fine-tuning benefits become clear.",A.ben,'What would a "quality bar" look like for an AI feature you care about? What would it need to reliably do?')
A.aV2=s([A.ahg,A.ahR,A.ai1],t.I2)
A.aia=new B.fA(14,"Fine-tune or Prompt?","When to teach the model vs when to ask it better.",A.aV2)
A.dM0=s(["First drafts of emails, replies, and summaries","A new email address","Deleting your inbox","Handwriting more letters"],t.s)
A.aei=new B.ap("The clearest early productivity win from AI is often\u2026",A.dM0,0,"Editing a rough draft is faster than staring at a blank compose window. Blank-page cost is real.")
A.aL8=s(["Read and edit it before sending","Send it immediately without reading","Delete it","Print and mail it"],t.s)
A.afY=new B.ap("When AI drafts a reply for you, you should\u2026",A.aL8,0,"AI drafts are starting points, not finished products. Signature, tone, facts \u2014 you still own the send button.")
A.bMO=s(["Whatever you paste may be used by the vendor for training","The AI will call your mother","It will change your email password","Nothing at all"],t.s)
A.agh=new B.ap("A privacy consideration when pasting emails into a public AI is\u2026",A.bMO,0,"Check the vendor policy. Enterprise tiers usually disable training on your data \u2014 consumer free tiers often do not.")
A.f6U=s([A.aei,A.afY,A.agh],t.Q)
A.ahb=new B.cq(1,"Email and inbox management.",'The lowest-friction wins are usually reading and writing help. Summarise a long thread; draft a first-pass reply; extract action items. Even average AI drafts save the "blank page" moment that costs the most time.',A.f6U,"What is one email or message you send this week that would be an obvious first-draft candidate for AI?")
A.eea=s(["A transcript, a summary, and a list of action items","A T-shirt","A calendar reset","A new laptop"],t.s)
A.afs=new B.ap("A common output from a modern AI meeting tool is\u2026",A.eea,0,"The pattern is standard now \u2014 most tools converge on those three artefacts.")
A.dLA=s(["Missing owners or due dates because they were never said out loud","Working too fast","Filing action items in French","Refusing to open"],t.s)
A.aez=new B.ap("A common failure of AI action-item extraction is\u2026",A.dLA,0,"The AI can only summarise what was actually said. Verbal clarity in the meeting is still worth the effort.")
A.cJ8=s(["Announce it and get consent, especially with external attendees","Never tell anyone","Only tell your manager","Only tell the AI"],t.s)
A.aeE=new B.ap("A polite baseline for AI note-taking is\u2026",A.cJ8,0,"Recording consent laws vary by state and country. Always flag it, and offer to disable if anyone objects.")
A.cYR=s([A.afs,A.aez,A.aeE],t.Q)
A.ahN=new B.cq(2,"Meetings: transcripts + smart summaries.","AI note-takers join a call, transcribe it, and produce searchable notes plus a list of action items and decisions. The catch: everyone in the room should know it is on, and sensitive meetings may need a policy about when to disable it.",A.cYR,"What kind of meeting would you want AI notes for, and which would you prefer stayed off-record?")
A.aWe=s(["Describe the calculation in plain English and get a formula back","Have the spreadsheet cook dinner","Delete cells randomly","Show a movie"],t.s)
A.agf=new B.ap("A natural-language formula in a spreadsheet lets you\u2026",A.aWe,0,"Great for the moment you know what you want but forgot the exact syntax.")
A.czo=s(["Accepting suggestions without checking that the numbers or facts are right","Missing the announcement banner","The tool becoming free","The screen getting brighter"],t.s)
A.agp=new B.ap("When AI is available inline in a tool, the biggest risk is\u2026",A.czo,0,"'Looks right' is not 'is right'. Especially with numbers.")
A.dNE=s(["The knowledge base is well-organised and up to date","The knowledge base is empty","The AI is offline","Nobody uses it"],t.s)
A.afh=new B.ap("Chatting with a knowledge base works best when\u2026",A.dNE,0,"AI cannot rescue a mess. Clean information architecture is what makes AI on top of it usable.")
A.cUg=s([A.agf,A.agp,A.afh],t.Q)
A.ahM=new B.cq(3,"Docs, spreadsheets, and knowledge work.","AI features are showing up inside every tool: Docs suggests rewrites, spreadsheets accept natural-language formulas, and knowledge bases can chat about their own contents. The skill to learn is where AI is inline vs where a standalone tool is still better.",A.cUg,"Where in your current toolset is AI already available that you haven't tried yet?")
A.bKS=s([A.ahb,A.ahN,A.ahM],t.I2)
A.aio=new B.fA(15,"AI at Work","Email, meetings, docs \u2014 the boring wins first.",A.bKS)
A.ex8=s(["The composition and labels of the training data","A bug in the transformer","The colour of the screen","The time of day"],t.s)
A.aeL=new B.ap("A primary source of bias in AI is\u2026",A.ex8,0,"Skewed data in, skewed patterns out. Debiasing usually means changing the data or the objective, not the layers.")
A.eGA=s(["It saw more high-quality examples of that group during training","It picks favourites","It reads horoscopes","It hates statistics"],t.s)
A.afx=new B.ap("A model can be more accurate for one group than another because\u2026",A.eGA,0,"Coverage matters. Under-represented cases get less accurate \u2014 that shows up as unfairness downstream.")
A.c_P=s(["Auditing the training data and evaluating per-group performance","Turning off the internet","Hiding the results","Renaming the model"],t.s)
A.afn=new B.ap("A first step to reducing bias is\u2026",A.c_P,0,"You cannot fix what you cannot measure. Per-group evals are the price of admission for anything consequential.")
A.f3k=s([A.aeL,A.afx,A.afn],t.Q)
A.ahC=new B.cq(1,"Bias comes from data, not the model choosing to be biased.",'A model reflects the patterns of its training data. If those data over-represent some groups or contexts, the model will be more accurate for them. This is not the model "wanting" to be biased \u2014 it just cannot outperform the evidence it saw.',A.f3k,"In a system you use, whose experience is likely worse than the average \u2014 and how would you find out?")
A.dNp=s(["A plausible-sounding statement that is not true","A visual artefact","A dropped connection","A hardware failure"],t.s)
A.af0=new B.ap("A hallucination in AI output is\u2026",A.dNp,0,"The output reads well precisely because the model was trained to sound fluent. Fluency and truth are separate.")
A.aJ8=s(["Ground the model in retrieved documents and ask for citations","Hope harder","Use ALL CAPS","Restart the app"],t.s)
A.aen=new B.ap("A concrete way to reduce hallucinations is\u2026",A.aJ8,0,'RAG plus citations plus explicit "I don\'t know" permission = fewer confident wrong answers.')
A.cy5=s(["The user cannot easily verify the answer and takes an action based on it","The model refuses to answer","The user is asking about jokes","The user has printed the reply"],t.s)
A.agJ=new B.ap("Hallucinations are most dangerous when\u2026",A.cy5,0,"Legal, medical, financial \u2014 anywhere action follows the answer, hallucinations have real cost.")
A.aZM=s([A.af0,A.aen,A.agJ],t.Q)
A.ahp=new B.cq(2,"Hallucinations: confident, plausible, wrong.",'Language models generate text that flows well whether or not the facts inside are true. A "hallucination" is when the output is fluent and confident but not grounded in reality. It is the single biggest source of AI mistakes in production.',A.aZM,"Have you ever caught an AI confidently making something up? What tipped you off?")
A.cQe=s(["A system deployed for a high-stakes decision without per-group evaluation","The AI going on strike","The AI winning an award","The AI running too fast"],t.s)
A.afH=new B.ap("A common theme in AI failure stories is\u2026",A.cQe,0,"Deploying without measuring \u2014 especially per-group \u2014 is how most public AI failures happen.")
A.aXB=s(["Making the AI explain its reasoning so humans can review it","Speeding it up","Hiding all logs","Removing the off switch"],t.s)
A.aef=new B.ap("A guardrail that reduces harm is\u2026",A.aXB,0,"Explainability plus accountability plus an override path = the minimum safe kit for consequential AI.")
A.eyL=s(["Pause the system, review the case, and update evals before redeploying","Deny it happened","Add more layers","Blame the intern"],t.s)
A.agC=new B.ap("When AI makes a mistake in production, the right first move is\u2026",A.eyL,0,"Incidents are the best learning material any team has. Waste them at your peril.")
A.egy=s([A.afH,A.aef,A.agC],t.Q)
A.ahy=new B.cq(3,"Real-world failures teach the most.","AI systems have caused real harms \u2014 recruiting tools that penalised women's CVs, facial recognition with worse accuracy on some skin tones, chatbots giving wrong legal advice. Every failure has the same shape: data limits meeting a decision the system was never rigorously evaluated for.",A.egy,"What is one AI decision in your industry that would benefit from a mandatory human review step?")
A.cAD=s([A.ahC,A.ahp,A.ahy],t.I2)
A.ai7=new B.fA(16,"Ethics + Bias","How AI systems go wrong \u2014 and why it matters.",A.cAD)
A.eWC=s(["The weights are not published \u2014 access is via a hosted API","You cannot chat with it","It only runs on weekends","It has no name"],t.s)
A.aeH=new B.ap("A closed model is one where\u2026",A.eWC,0,"You pay per token, you never see the weights, but you also never have to run inference yourself.")
A.bkL=s(["The ability to run and modify the model on your own hardware","Free hosting from the vendor","Guaranteed higher accuracy","A refund"],t.s)
A.ag8=new B.ap("An open-weight model gives you\u2026",A.bkL,0,'You own the deployment. That is what makes it "yours" \u2014 and it is why some industries prefer it.')
A.e3J=s(["Data cannot leave the company or a specific region","You dislike menus","Open models are always faster","Closed models require a fax"],t.s)
A.aee=new B.ap("A common reason to choose an open model is\u2026",A.e3J,0,"Data residency, regulated industries, and offline requirements all push toward open weights.")
A.ckx=s([A.aeH,A.ag8,A.aee],t.Q)
A.ahI=new B.cq(1,"Closed models are convenient. Open models are yours.","Closed hosted models (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google) are fastest to try \u2014 one API key and you are done. Open-weight models (Llama, Mistral, and others) can be downloaded, run on your own infrastructure, and modified. The tradeoff is convenience vs control.",A.ckx,"Would your work data be OK sitting on a US-based hosted API, or would you need it to stay on your own machines?")
A.eTM=s(["The number of input and output tokens","The colour of your logo","Your monthly rent","The weather"],t.s)
A.ah_=new B.ap("Hosted API pricing typically scales with\u2026",A.eTM,0,"Read the pricing sheet carefully. Input and output tokens often cost different amounts.")
A.em3=s(["Enough GPU capacity and someone to keep it running","A single laptop","A calculator","A subscription to Word"],t.s)
A.af6=new B.ap("Self-hosting a large open model requires\u2026",A.em3,0,"This is a real infrastructure commitment. Budget for people as well as hardware.")
A.bde=s(["Steady, high-volume traffic where API costs exceed hardware costs","Any traffic at all","One user per month","Traffic on Sundays only"],t.s)
A.afP=new B.ap("A crossover point where self-hosting starts to pay off is\u2026",A.bde,0,"Model this before committing. Volume determines whether the maths bends toward hosted or self-hosted.")
A.aNE=s([A.ah_,A.af6,A.afP],t.Q)
A.ahe=new B.cq(2,"Cost, latency, and total ownership.","Hosted APIs charge per token and scale up automatically. Self-hosted open models have zero marginal per-request cost but require GPUs, engineering, and ops. For heavy usage the crossover point can favour self-hosting; for light usage the API almost always wins.",A.aNE,"For a hypothetical AI feature you shipped, how would you decide when to move from hosted API to self-hosted?")
A.eTO=s(["Read the licence and check any usage restrictions","Ignore it and hope","Ask a friend","Only use it on Fridays"],t.s)
A.ae2=new B.ap("A safe habit before using an open model commercially is\u2026",A.eTO,0,"Some open models come with clauses about very large user bases. Others are fully permissive.")
A.bVp=s(["You can experiment but not ship it in a commercial product","You must publish everything","Everyone gets equal royalties","The model works only in laboratories"],t.s)
A.aep=new B.ap('A "research-only" model licence typically means\u2026',A.bVp,0,"Great for internal exploration. Not great as your product foundation without a commercial licence.")
A.cyb=s(["Benchmark scores, licence terms, and community adoption","The vendor's office plants","The colour of the download button","Alphabetical order"],t.s)
A.afN=new B.ap("When comparing open models, useful signals include\u2026",A.cyb,0,"Adoption drives ecosystem \u2014 tooling, fine-tunes, blog posts. Boring signals but decisive ones.")
A.eQj=s([A.ae2,A.aep,A.afN],t.Q)
A.ahl=new B.cq(3,"Licences: read them.","Not all 'open' models are open in the same way. Some allow commercial use freely, some restrict it above a certain user count, and some are research-only. Before building a business on top of a model, read its licence and check the vendor is a stable enough entity to keep providing it.",A.eQj,"If you had to bet a product on one AI vendor for the next three years, what would you want to know before signing?")
A.b6C=s([A.ahI,A.ahe,A.ahl],t.I2)
A.aic=new B.fA(17,"Open vs Closed","The tradeoffs between hosted APIs and open-weight models.",A.b6C)
A.d2B=s(["Faster boilerplate and less context switching to documentation","Shorter meetings","Silent keyboards","A brand new IDE theme"],t.s)
A.afR=new B.ap("The main productivity gain from AI autocomplete is\u2026",A.d2B,0,"You stay in the editor. You stay in flow. The docs tab gets opened much less often.")
A.bNH=s(["Introducing subtle bugs or insecure patterns you did not review","Better performance","Automatic tests","Increased salary"],t.s)
A.agX=new B.ap("A risk of accepting AI code suggestions blindly is\u2026",A.bNH,0,"Confident wrongness in code is more expensive than in prose. Reading suggestions is part of the workflow.")
A.dMX=s(["Robust tests and code review, so bad suggestions get caught","Never testing again","Turning off git","Renaming variables randomly"],t.s)
A.afZ=new B.ap("A good habit alongside AI-assisted coding is\u2026",A.dMX,0,"The tests were the guardrail before AI. They are more of a guardrail now.")
A.dqa=s([A.afR,A.agX,A.afZ],t.Q)
A.aht=new B.cq(1,"Inline suggestions changed daily coding first.",'The first AI coding tools were autocomplete: as you type, the model suggests the next line or block. This dropped a real chunk of time for boilerplate \u2014 routes, tests, DTOs \u2014 and trained a whole generation of engineers to think in "accept or edit" mode.',A.dqa,"When AI autocompletes something in a file, do you tend to read it carefully, or just accept and move on?")
A.dNa=s(["Chat with the codebase and stage multi-file diffs","Ban all comments","Prevent saving files","Delete git history"],t.s)
A.afw=new B.ap("An AI-first editor lets you\u2026",A.dNa,0,"The editor becomes a review surface. You read diffs the AI wrote, not lines you typed.")
A.dpg=s(["Bigger diffs are harder to review than small ones","The screen goes dark","You lose access to your keyboard","The editor gets slower"],t.s)
A.agY=new B.ap("A downside of larger AI edits is\u2026",A.dpg,0,"Big AI diffs mask small AI mistakes. Prefer many small reviewable edits over one giant one.")
A.eM8=s(["Commit small logical chunks so each change is reversible","Push straight to production","Skip commit messages","Turn off the linter"],t.s)
A.agx=new B.ap("A useful discipline with AI edits is\u2026",A.eM8,0,"Small, well-labelled commits are your friend when AI is writing chunks of the codebase.")
A.eY4=s([A.afw,A.agY,A.agx],t.Q)
A.ahx=new B.cq(2,"AI-first editors and multi-file edits.",'Newer editors treat AI as a first-class collaborator. You chat with your codebase, ask the AI to change several files at once, and it stages a diff you can review. Cursor is a prominent example; there are others. The workflow shift is from "line-by-line" to "step-by-step".',A.eY4,"If your editor could reliably make cross-file changes on your description, which chore would you delegate first?")
A.exE=s(["Plans, runs commands, reads the output, and iterates","Only writes commit messages","Only replaces the mouse","Only runs on Tuesdays"],t.s)
A.aeu=new B.ap("A terminal coding agent typically\u2026",A.exE,0,"The loop of plan / act / observe / adjust is what makes it feel like real assistance instead of just autocomplete.")
A.exW=s(["A clear task, a well-tested project, and permission to run tools","No task at all","A completely empty repo","One infinite loop"],t.s)
A.aed=new B.ap("Coding agents work best when they have\u2026",A.exW,0,"Tests + type checks + a clear brief = the agent has ways to know when it is done.")
A.dLh=s(["Requiring human confirmation on irreversible actions like force pushes","Removing all confirmations","Giving them root by default","Hiding the terminal"],t.s)
A.ae5=new B.ap("A guardrail for coding agents is\u2026",A.dLh,0,"Same principle as any AI agent \u2014 irreversible = confirm. Reversible = let it run.")
A.bWt=s([A.aeu,A.aed,A.ae5],t.Q)
A.ahc=new B.cq(3,"Coding agents in the terminal.","The next step is coding agents that live in your terminal \u2014 Claude Code and similar tools. They read your codebase, plan changes, run commands, and iterate on the result. You review, accept, or push back. It is closer to pair programming than autocomplete.",A.bWt,"What is one task in your workflow that a coding agent could do overnight while you sleep?")
A.evc=s([A.aht,A.ahx,A.ahc],t.I2)
A.aih=new B.fA(18,"AI + Code","Copilots, agents, and the changing shape of engineering.",A.evc)
A.enD=s(["Training is a one-off huge run; inference is the ongoing serving cost","Training happens at night, inference by day","Only training uses GPUs","They are actually the same thing"],t.s)
A.ae6=new B.ap("Training and inference differ because\u2026",A.enD,0,"CFOs care about both, but they show up on different lines of the P&L.")
A.ber=s(["Inference, over time, than on the initial training","Ribbon cutting","Office lunches","Domain names"],t.s)
A.afa=new B.ap("A widely used model can spend more on\u2026",A.ber,0,"Millions of users x billions of tokens adds up. Inference cost becomes the dominant line.")
A.f6f=s(["Batching many requests through the GPU together","Turning off the datacenter","Buying a slower GPU","Removing all users"],t.s)
A.afi=new B.ap("A common inference optimisation is\u2026",A.f6f,0,"Batching, quantisation, and speculative decoding each cut inference cost by real percentages.")
A.cTH=s([A.ae6,A.afa,A.afi],t.Q)
A.ai2=new B.cq(1,"Training vs inference: two very different bills.","Training a frontier model is a one-off, huge cost \u2014 thousands of GPUs running for weeks or months. Inference is the day-to-day cost of running the trained model to serve users. For a widely used model, inference can eventually exceed the original training bill.",A.cTH,"If you owned an AI product, would you optimise training or inference first? Why?")
A.dTX=s(["They are excellent at parallel matrix multiplication","They have flashy lights","They boot faster than CPUs","They are quieter"],t.s)
A.afu=new B.ap("GPUs are used for AI because\u2026",A.dTX,0,"The maths behind neural nets is mostly matrix operations. GPUs do that in parallel across thousands of cores.")
A.b64=s(["GPU capacity is limited \u2014 securing enough chips is a real challenge","GPUs are given away for free","Frontier AI runs on phones by default","GPUs are illegal"],t.s)
A.agU=new B.ap("A supply-side reality of frontier AI is\u2026",A.b64,0,"Data centre GPU capacity has been a headline constraint for years. Cloud contracts often turn on it.")
A.dRM=s(["A custom AI accelerator originally developed by Google","A type of USB drive","A file format","A software licence"],t.s)
A.afq=new B.ap("A TPU is\u2026",A.dRM,0,"Tensor Processing Unit. Google built them for their own AI workloads and also sells access on Google Cloud.")
A.blw=s([A.afu,A.agU,A.afq],t.Q)
A.ahd=new B.cq(2,"GPUs, TPUs, and specialised silicon.","The main hardware behind modern AI is the GPU \u2014 originally built for graphics, well suited to the matrix maths deep learning needs. Google has its own alternative (TPUs), and several companies are building specialised AI accelerators. Supply of these chips is one of the biggest bottlenecks in the industry.",A.blw,"How does hardware scarcity affect the AI features you personally use \u2014 via price, speed, or availability?")
A.dRv=s(["Using lower-precision numbers for the weights and activations","Sending fewer emails","Adding more layers","Doubling the data"],t.s)
A.ae_=new B.ap("Quantisation makes models cheaper to run by\u2026",A.dRv,0,"Trading a fraction of accuracy for meaningful memory + compute savings is one of the biggest levers we have.")
A.dUu=s(["Training a smaller model to imitate a larger one","Removing water from datasets","Cutting features from a product","Adding more parameters"],t.s)
A.afm=new B.ap("Distillation is the technique of\u2026",A.dUu,0,"The student model is faster and cheaper. If it is close enough on quality, you keep the win.")
A.bMc=s(["Predicting several tokens ahead and verifying them in parallel","Guessing your password","Refusing hard questions","Skipping the model entirely"],t.s)
A.ae3=new B.ap("Speculative decoding speeds up generation by\u2026",A.bMc,0,"The maths is elegant and the wins are real \u2014 many popular inference stacks use it now.")
A.baC=s([A.ae_,A.afm,A.ae3],t.Q)
A.ai0=new B.cq(3,"Efficiency compounds.","A small percentage improvement in model efficiency is a big deal at planetary scale. Techniques like quantisation, distillation, and speculative decoding all trade a little accuracy or complexity for large cost or latency wins. Over a year, efficiency work often saves more money than model-size upgrades.",A.baC,"In your own work, when have you traded a little quality for a large gain in speed or cost? Would you do it again?")
A.eqI=s([A.ai2,A.ahd,A.ai0],t.I2)
A.aib=new B.fA(19,"The Economics of AI","Where the money goes: training, inference, and GPUs.",A.eqI)
A.aYS=s(["Longer effective context and better multi-step reasoning","Shorter outputs and less capability","Removing tool use","Making models forget faster"],t.s)
A.agH=new B.ap("Which is a clear direction in current AI research?",A.aYS,0,"Context windows have grown from thousands to millions of tokens in a few years. Reasoning benchmarks keep climbing.")
A.e45=s(["Small improvements compounding across many tasks","One single breakthrough that changes everything overnight","Complete stagnation","AI becoming completely free"],t.s)
A.afM=new B.ap("A safer bet for the near future is\u2026",A.e45,0,"Cinematic single-day breakthroughs are rare. Compounding improvements are the actual pattern.")
A.eS0=s(["A rising tide that lifts many boats unevenly","A single boat racing alone","A flat sea","A boat that never leaves the dock"],t.s)
A.afA=new B.ap("A useful mental model for AI progress is\u2026",A.eS0,0,"Some fields benefit sooner, some later, and the leader model changes over time. Bet on the trend, not one vendor.")
A.cj2=s([A.agH,A.afM,A.afA],t.Q)
A.ahH=new B.cq(1,"Better reasoning, longer context, more autonomy.","The clearest direction is toward models that reason more carefully, remember longer conversations and larger documents, and act more autonomously within scoped tasks. The pace of change on these three fronts has been steady for years, and the next few years will keep the trend going.",A.cj2,"Which of your daily tasks do you think will be substantially affected by AI in the next two years?")
A.eLy=s(["Higher accuracy on a narrow task at a lower cost","Bragging rights only","Longer training time","It is illegal not to"],t.s)
A.aeA=new B.ap("A reason to use a specialised model is\u2026",A.eLy,0,"Specialisation pays. A tiny model tuned for one thing often beats a giant model asked to do everything.")
A.d2g=s(["A general model orchestrating a set of specialised tools and models","One monolithic model doing every step alone","No models at all","A human doing every step manually forever"],t.s)
A.agN=new B.ap("A likely production pattern is\u2026",A.d2g,0,"This is already how many real systems are built. Expect more of it.")
A.cJ3=s(["You can update or swap individual components without redoing the whole system","They are simpler than a single model","They are always cheaper by default","They run without electricity"],t.s)
A.afz=new B.ap("A benefit of hybrid systems is\u2026",A.cJ3,0,"Composable systems age better. You can replace one piece without a full rebuild.")
A.eLF=s([A.aeA,A.agN,A.afz],t.Q)
A.ahD=new B.cq(2,"Specialised beats generic for many jobs.","Frontier general-purpose models get all the headlines, but a lot of production value comes from smaller, task-specific models \u2014 medical imaging, legal drafting, protein folding. Expect a healthy ecosystem where a general model is the brain and specialised models handle the hard specific bits.",A.eLF,"In your industry, what is one specialised AI capability that would move the needle more than a bigger general model?")
A.dLn=s(["Judgement about which outputs to trust and which to reject","Being able to type faster","Never using AI at all","Knowing the model version number"],t.s)
A.aeQ=new B.ap("A durable skill in an AI-heavy workplace is\u2026",A.dLn,0,"Critical review is where humans stay valuable. Volume of output is where AI stays valuable.")
A.eSd=s(["Using them when they help and setting them aside when they do not","Using them for every possible task","Refusing to try them","Blaming them for everything"],t.s)
A.agl=new B.ap("A healthy relationship with AI tools involves\u2026",A.eSd,0,"It is a tool, not a religion. Fit for purpose, or nothing.")
A.bMV=s(["How to frame problems clearly and evaluate answers","The exact hyperparameters of the current top model","How to draw perfect circles","Which vendor has the largest logo"],t.s)
A.aeM=new B.ap("The most important thing to keep learning as AI evolves is\u2026",A.bMV,0,"Fashionable model names come and go. Framing and evaluation are the evergreen skills.")
A.dNL=s([A.aeQ,A.agl,A.aeM],t.Q)
A.ahU=new B.cq(3,"The skill is judgement, not typing prompts.",'As AI absorbs the mechanical parts of many jobs, the human edge shifts to judgement: framing the right problem, reviewing outputs critically, deciding what is good enough, and knowing when to say no. The skills that will age well are less about "prompting" and more about clear thinking.',A.dNL,"Twenty weeks in \u2014 what is the single most valuable AI habit you want to keep after finishing this course?")
A.bTa=s([A.ahH,A.ahD,A.ahU],t.I2)
