A.lBz=new B.r("Coca-Cola registers its famous contour bottle patent",1915,"The Root Glass Company shaped the bottle so it could be recognised by touch in the dark or by its pieces on the ground. The design patent was granted in 1915.")
A.lxg=new B.r("Toblerone is created in Bern, Switzerland",1908,"Theodor Tobler and Emil Baumann combined honey and almond nougat into the triangular chocolate bar. Its shape is often linked to Bern's Matterhorn-inspired peaks.")
A.lww=new B.r("Nathan's Famous opens its first hot dog stand on Coney Island",1916,"Nathan Handwerker sold hot dogs for a nickel, undercutting rivals. The Brooklyn stand later spawned an annual July 4th eating contest.")
A.lvu=new B.r("Wonder Bread launches in the United States",1921,"It arrived pre-packaged and, from 1930, pre-sliced \u2014 helping popularise the phrase 'the best thing since sliced bread'.")
A.lB3=new B.r("The Milky Way chocolate bar goes on sale",1923,"Frank Mars filled a chocolate bar with malted nougat to mimic a malted milkshake. It became one of the first mass-market filled bars.")
A.lzf=new B.r("Kool-Aid powdered drink mix is introduced",1927,"Edwin Perkins reworked a liquid concentrate into a lightweight powder that was cheaper to ship. Packets sold for a dime during the Depression.")
A.lw5=new B.r("7 Up is first sold in the United States",1929,"Launched just before the Wall Street Crash, it was originally marketed as a lithiated lemon-lime tonic before the recipe was simplified.")
A.lzV=new B.r("Snickers is launched by Mars",1930,"The bar was named after a favourite horse owned by the Mars family. It pairs nougat, caramel and peanuts in a chocolate coating.")
A.lC4=new B.r("Toll House chocolate chip cookies are created in Massachusetts",1938,"Ruth Wakefield chopped a chocolate bar into a cookie dough at her Toll House Inn. The recipe was later printed on Nestl\xe9 chip packages.")
A.lvB=new B.r("Spam canned meat is introduced by Hormel",1937,"The shelf-stable pork product became a wartime staple shipped around the world and remains especially popular in Hawaii and parts of Asia.")
A.lCk=new B.r("KFC's founder Harland Sanders begins selling fried chicken",1930,"Sanders cooked for travellers at his Corbin, Kentucky service station. He was named an honorary Kentucky Colonel in 1935 and franchised the recipe in the 1950s.")
A.lzv=new B.r("M&M's chocolate candies go on sale in the US",1941,"The hard sugar shell was designed to stop the chocolate melting in soldiers' hands. The sweets were first sold to the US military.")
A.lw8=new B.r("The first McDonald's drive-in opens in San Bernardino, California",1948,"Brothers Richard and Maurice McDonald relaunched their diner with a streamlined 'Speedee Service System' and a short menu built around the 15-cent hamburger.")
A.lCi=new B.r("Nutella's forerunner spread is created in Italy",1946,"Pietro Ferrero mixed hazelnuts into scarce post-war chocolate to make a paste called Giandujot. It was reworked into a spreadable cream and renamed Nutella in 1964.")
A.lu7=new B.r("Cheetos snacks are launched in the United States",1948,"Fritos maker Charles Elmer Doolin developed the puffed cornmeal snack, which was distributed nationally by the 1950s.")
A.lzK=new B.r("The first Denny's-style coffee shop, Danny's Donuts, opens",1953,"The California diner started as a doughnut stand before growing into a 24-hour restaurant chain and changing its name to Denny's in 1959.")
A.lBO=new B.r("Ray Kroc opens his first McDonald's franchise in Des Plaines, Illinois",1955,"Kroc's Illinois restaurant kickstarted the national franchise. The company later marked this location as the start of the modern chain.")
A.lCJ=new B.r("Instant ramen is invented in Japan",1958,"Momofuku Ando of Nissin created 'Chicken Ramen' by flash-frying pre-cooked noodles so they could be revived with hot water. It reshaped convenience eating worldwide.")
A.lAi=new B.r("Pizza Hut opens its first restaurant in Wichita, Kansas",1958,"Brothers Dan and Frank Carney borrowed money from their mother to start the parlour. The sign had room for only eight letters, which shaped the name.")
A.lx9=new B.r("The first sushi bar for a Western clientele opens in the US",1966,"Kawafuku in Los Angeles is often cited as an early Little Tokyo sushi bar serving American diners, helping seed sushi's later spread across the West.")
A.lxo=new B.r("The Big Mac is added to the McDonald's menu nationwide",1968,"Jim Delligatti had already been selling it in Pittsburgh since 1967. The 'two all-beef patties' jingle came out in 1974.")
A.lvL=new B.r("The Pringles stacked crisp is launched in the US",1968,"Procter & Gamble engineered a saddle-shaped chip that stacked neatly in a tube, cutting down on breakage and air in the packaging.")
A.lA7=new B.r("The first Starbucks store opens in Seattle's Pike Place Market",1971,"The original shop sold roasted beans and equipment rather than brewed drinks. The espresso-bar model came after Howard Schultz joined in the 1980s.")
A.lvS=new B.r("Pot Noodle goes on sale in the United Kingdom",1977,"The instant snack pot became a British cupboard staple. It was based on the wider boom in add-hot-water noodle products from Asia.")
A.lCr=new B.r("Ben & Jerry's opens its first scoop shop in Vermont",1978,"Ben Cohen and Jerry Greenfield set up in a converted petrol station after taking a mail-order course in ice cream making.")
A.lw1=new B.r("H\xe4agen-Dazs opens its first dedicated shop",1976,"The brand, founded in the Bronx in 1961, used an invented Scandinavian-sounding name to suggest old-world craftsmanship. Its first franchised shop opened in Brooklyn.")
A.lBN=new B.r("Chicken McNuggets roll out across McDonald's in the US",1983,"Developed with help from food scientists, the nuggets came in four shapes and quickly became one of the chain's best-selling items.")
A.lA3=new B.r("Diet Coke launches in the United States",1982,"It was the first new brand to use the Coca-Cola name since 1886 and rapidly became one of the best-selling soft drinks in America.")
A.lx1=new B.r("Red Bull energy drink launches in Austria",1987,"Dietrich Mateschitz adapted a Thai tonic drink called Krating Daeng for European tastes, creating the modern energy-drink category.")
A.lwG=new B.r("The first British McDonald's Drive-Thru opens",1986,"Fife's Drive-Thru brought the American format to the UK, letting customers order without leaving the car \u2014 a novelty for many British drivers at the time.")
A.lyy=new B.r("Nando's opens its first restaurant in Johannesburg",1987,"The Portuguese-style peri-peri chicken chain started in South Africa and later expanded across the UK, Australia and beyond.")
A.lvJ=new B.r("Starbucks opens its first store outside North America, in Tokyo",1996,"The Ginza store marked the brand's international push. Japan became one of its largest markets outside the United States.")
A.luf=new B.r("Innocent smoothies go on sale in the UK",1999,"The founders famously tested the idea at a music festival, asking people to bin empty cups under 'yes' or 'no' signs to decide whether to quit their jobs.")
A.lve=new B.r("Pret A Manger opens its first US store, in New York",2000,"The British sandwich chain, founded in London in 1986, brought its made-fresh-daily model to Manhattan around the turn of the millennium.")
A.lBU=new B.r("Krispy Kreme lists on the stock market amid a doughnut boom",2000,"The Southern US doughnut brand, founded in 1937, saw huge demand as it expanded nationally, with its 'Hot Now' sign signalling fresh batches.")
A.lwi=new B.r("Monster Energy drink launches in the US",2002,"Hansen Natural entered the fast-growing energy category with a larger can, quickly becoming one of Red Bull's main competitors.")
A.lzg=new B.r("The cronut pastry debuts in New York",2013,"Chef Dominique Ansel's croissant-doughnut hybrid drew queues around the block. He trademarked the name to distinguish it from imitations.")
A.lwc=new B.r("Oatly's oat milk arrives widely in coffee shops in the US",2016,"The Swedish brand, whose oat drink dates to the 1990s, gained traction with baristas thanks to a version made to steam and foam like dairy milk.")
A.lBD=new B.r("The Impossible Burger makes its restaurant debut in New York",2016,"The plant-based patty used an iron-carrying molecule called heme to mimic the taste and browning of beef. It launched at a handful of US restaurants.")
A.lAx=new B.r("Burger King launches the Impossible Whopper across the US",2019,"After a St. Louis trial earlier in the year, the plant-based Whopper rolled out nationwide, marking a big fast-food move into meat alternatives.")
A.lv8=new B.r("A vegan restaurant earns a Michelin star in France",2021,"ONA in Ar\xe8s became the first fully plant-based restaurant in France to receive a Michelin star, a notable moment for meat-free fine dining.")
A.lBA=new B.r("Prime hydration drink launches and sells out widely",2022,"Founded by two online creators, the sports drink drew long queues and resale markups in the UK and US as demand outstripped supply.")
A.Nw=s([A.lBz,A.lxg,A.lww,A.lvu,A.lB3,A.lzf,A.lw5,A.lzV,A.lC4,A.lvB,A.lCk,A.lzv,A.lw8,A.lCi,A.lu7,A.lzK,A.lBO,A.lCJ,A.lAi,A.lx9,A.lxo,A.lvL,A.lA7,A.lvS,A.lCr,A.lw1,A.lBN,A.lA3,A.lx1,A.lwG,A.lyy,A.lvJ,A.luf,A.lve,A.lBU,A.lwi,A.lzg,A.lwc,A.lBD,A.lAx,A.lv8,A.lBA],t.CV)
A.ly4=new B.r("The Paris M\xe9tro opens its first underground line",1900,"Line 1 opened in time for the 1900 World's Fair. The elegant green Art Nouveau station entrances became a symbol of Paris in their own right.")
A.lA6=new B.r("The Flatiron Building rises in New York City",1902,"Its narrow triangular shape fits a wedge-shaped block on Fifth Avenue. At 22 storeys it was one of the tallest steel-framed buildings of its day.")
A.lvf=new B.r("The first New York City subway line opens",1904,"The original route ran 9 miles and cost a nickel to ride. Over a hundred thousand people packed the trains on the very first day.")
A.lzc=new B.r("The Singer Building becomes the world's tallest building",1908,"At 187 metres it briefly held the world record in Manhattan. The skyscraper race it kicked off would reshape city skylines for decades.")
A.lz8=new B.r("The Woolworth Building opens as the world's tallest",1913,"Nicknamed the 'Cathedral of Commerce' for its Gothic styling, it held the tallest-building title for 17 years \u2014 a long reign in the fast-moving skyscraper era.")
A.lA_=new B.r("The Panama Canal opens between the Atlantic and Pacific",1914,"The canal shaved thousands of miles off the sea route around South America. A system of giant locks lifts ships up and over the continental divide.")
A.lzj=new B.r("The Grand Canyon becomes a US National Park",1919,"The canyon is over 400 km long and up to 1.8 km deep. The rock at the very bottom is nearly two billion years old.")
A.lAA=new B.r("The Lincoln Memorial is dedicated in Washington",1922,"The seated marble figure of Lincoln is nearly 6 metres tall. If he stood up, the statue would be around 8.5 metres high.")
A.lyD=new B.r("The Holland Tunnel opens under the Hudson River",1927,"It was the first underwater road tunnel with a mechanical ventilation system. Huge fans swap the air inside every 90 seconds so cars can drive safely below the river.")
A.lyf=new B.r("The Cyclone roller coaster opens at Coney Island",1927,"This wooden coaster is still running today, nearly a century on. Its steep first drop and rattling turns made it one of the most famous rides in the world.")
A.lCF=new B.r("The Chrysler Building becomes the world's tallest",1930,"Its shining steel crown was assembled in secret inside the building and hoisted into place to snatch the height record. It held the title for less than a year.")
A.lvb=new B.r("The Empire State Building opens in New York",1931,"It was built in just 410 days and stood as the world's tallest for nearly 40 years. Around 3,400 workers raced up the frame at a floor or more per day.")
A.lue=new B.r("The George Washington Bridge opens over the Hudson",1931,"It doubled the length of the longest bridge span in the world when it opened. The towers were meant to be clad in stone, but the bare steel look was kept \u2014 and admired.")
A.lCa=new B.r("The Sydney Harbour Bridge opens in Australia",1932,"Locals nickname it 'the Coathanger' for its great steel arch. Around six million hand-driven rivets hold it together.")
A.luE=new B.r("The Hoover Dam is completed on the Colorado River",1936,"At 221 metres it was the tallest dam in the world at the time. It created Lake Mead, one of the largest reservoirs on the continent.")
A.lv3=new B.r("The carving of Mount Rushmore is completed",1941,"Four presidents' faces were blasted and chiselled into a granite cliff over 14 years. Each head is around 18 metres tall.")
A.lzO=new B.r("The Pentagon is completed near Washington",1943,"It remains one of the largest office buildings on Earth. Its five-sided design means no two points inside are more than a seven-minute walk apart.")
A.lw7=new B.r("Edmund Hillary and Tenzing Norgay first summit Everest",1953,"They reached the 8,849-metre summit and spent about 15 minutes at the top of the world. News reached London just in time for the Queen's coronation.")
A.lxH=new B.r("The Atomium opens for the Brussels World's Fair",1958,"It is shaped like an iron crystal magnified 165 billion times. Visitors ride escalators and a lift through its nine giant connected spheres.")
A.lAZ=new B.r("The first overland crossing of Antarctica is completed",1958,"Vivian Fuchs led the Commonwealth expedition 3,500 km across the frozen continent using tracked vehicles. It was the first time anyone had crossed Antarctica by land.")
A.lzH=new B.r("The Guggenheim Museum opens in New York",1959,"Frank Lloyd Wright designed it as a single spiralling ramp, so visitors wind gently downward past the art. Its white curves stand out against Manhattan's straight lines.")
A.lu9=new B.r("The Space Needle opens for the Seattle World's Fair",1962,"It was built to withstand high winds and earthquakes, and topped with a flying-saucer observation deck. The whole tower went up in under a year.")
A.lvs=new B.r("The Gateway Arch is completed in St. Louis",1965,"At 192 metres it is the tallest arch in the world. A small tram carries visitors up through the hollow curve to windows at the very top.")
A.lxa=new B.r("The Sydney Opera House opens in Australia",1973,"Its sail-like shells took 14 years to design and build. The white roof is covered in more than a million tiles that seem to change colour with the light.")
A.lub=new B.r("The Sears Tower in Chicago becomes the world's tallest",1973,"Now called the Willis Tower, it held the record for 25 years. Its glass-floored 'ledge' boxes let visitors look straight down more than 400 metres.")
A.lun=new B.r("The CN Tower opens in Toronto",1976,"At 553 metres it was the world's tallest free-standing structure for over 30 years. Its glass floor lets brave visitors stand on clear panels high above the city.")
A.lzP=new B.r("The Humber Bridge opens in England",1981,"For 16 years it had the longest single suspension span in the world. The towers are actually built slightly out of parallel to allow for the curve of the Earth.")
A.lz6=new B.r("The Thames Barrier is officially opened in London",1984,"Its shining steel gates rise from the riverbed to protect the city from surging tides. When not in use the huge gates lie flat, out of sight beneath the water.")
A.lv2=new B.r("The Eiffel Tower gets its golden nighttime lighting",1985,"A new scheme of lamps was fitted inside the frame to light it from within, giving its warm golden glow. The famous sparkle effect was added years later for the millennium.")
A.lzs=new B.r("The glass Pyramid opens at the Louvre in Paris",1989,"Designed by architect I. M. Pei, it forms a modern main entrance in the museum's grand old courtyard. It is made of hundreds of diamond and triangle glass panes.")
A.lwk=new B.r(u.h,1994,"The tunnel runs 50 km, most of it deep under the sea. Teams digging from both countries met in the middle within a few centimetres of each other.")
A.lwl=new B.r("The Petronas Towers become the world's tallest buildings",1998,"The twin towers in Kuala Lumpur are linked by a sky bridge near the halfway point. Their design draws on Islamic geometric patterns.")
A.luL=new B.r("The Akashi Kaiky\u014d Bridge opens in Japan",1998,"It has the longest central span of any suspension bridge, at nearly two kilometres. It was built to withstand fierce sea winds and powerful earthquakes.")
A.lzY=new B.r("The London Eye opens on the River Thames",2000,"At 135 metres it was the tallest Ferris wheel in the world when it opened. Each glass capsule turns so slowly the wheel never has to stop for passengers to step on.")
A.lzz=new B.r("Taipei 101 becomes the world's tallest building",2004,"The tower is shaped like a stalk of bamboo in eight tiers. A huge golden ball hangs near the top and swings to steady the building in high winds.")
A.lu4=new B.r("The Millau Viaduct opens in southern France",2004,"It is the tallest bridge in the world, with one mast rising higher than the Eiffel Tower. On a misty day the road seems to float above the clouds in the valley below.")
A.luj=new B.r("Beijing's National Stadium, the \"Bird's Nest\", is completed",2008,"Its lattice of criss-crossing steel beams gives it the look of a woven nest. It was built as the centrepiece for the Beijing Olympic Games.")
A.lC7=new B.r("The Burj Khalifa opens as the world's tallest building",2010,"At 828 metres it edges out Taipei 101 by more than 300 metres. On a clear day you can watch the sunset from the base, then ride up and watch it set a second time.")
A.lCC=new B.r("The Shard opens as the tallest building in Western Europe",2012,"Its glass-clad spire in London tapers to a jagged point, like a shard of glass. The viewing gallery near the top looks out over 40 miles on a clear day.")
A.lz4=new B.r("The Shanghai Tower is completed as China's tallest building",2015,"At 632 metres it twists gently as it rises, a shape that helps it shrug off strong winds. It holds one of the fastest lifts in the world, climbing at over 20 metres a second.")
A.lBn=new B.r("Merdeka 118 opens in Kuala Lumpur",2024,"At 679 metres it became the world's second-tallest building. Its glass facade is made of angular panels inspired by traditional Malaysian patterns.")
