A.lvn=new B.r("The first Tour de France cycling race is held",1903,"Just 60 riders set off, tackling six mammoth stages \u2014 some over 400 km through the night. A newspaper dreamed it up to sell more copies, and it worked spectacularly.")
A.lvT=new B.r("The London Olympics fix the marathon distance at 26.2 miles",1908,"The course was stretched to 26 miles 385 yards so it could finish in front of the royal box. That slightly odd number became the official marathon distance worldwide.")
A.luW=new B.r("The first Indianapolis 500 motor race is run",1911,"Winner Ray Harroun bolted a small mirror to his car so he could race without a riding mechanic watching behind him \u2014 an early ancestor of the rear-view mirror.")
A.lwy=new B.r("The Stockholm Olympics introduce photo-finish and electric timing",1912,"For the first time, cameras and electric clocks helped settle who crossed the line first. Before that, judges simply eyeballed the finish and argued it out.")
A.lwo=new B.r("The five-ring Olympic flag flies for the first time, in Antwerp",1920,"The now-famous rings had been designed years earlier but war delayed their debut. The athletes' oath was also sworn for the very first time at these Games.")
A.lzS=new B.r("Abrahams and Liddell win Olympic sprint golds in Paris",1924,"Their story later became the film Chariots of Fire. Eric Liddell refused to race on a Sunday, switched events, and won the 400m instead of his favoured 100m.")
A.luS=new B.r("Babe Ruth hits 60 home runs in a single baseball season",1927,"It was more home runs than most entire teams managed that year. The record stood for 34 seasons until Roger Maris passed it in 1961.")
A.lxV=new B.r("Women compete in Olympic athletics for the first time, in Amsterdam",1928,"These Games also saw Coca-Cola's first Olympic sponsorship \u2014 it shipped 1,000 cases over with the US team, starting a partnership that still runs today.")
A.lwe=new B.r("The Los Angeles Olympics introduce the three-tier victory podium",1932,"Gold, silver and bronze had never been raised to different heights before. The Games also built the first purpose-made Olympic Village to house the athletes.")
A.lBM=new B.r("Jesse Owens wins four gold medals at the Berlin Olympics",1936,"He took the 100m, 200m, long jump and relay in a single week. A German rival, Luz Long, openly offered him jumping advice and the two became lifelong friends.")
A.lAG=new B.r("Italy wins its second consecutive football World Cup",1938,"Held in France, it was the last World Cup for 12 years \u2014 the next two were cancelled. Only 15 nations took part, several arriving by steamship.")
A.lCc=new B.r("Jackie Robinson breaks baseball's colour barrier",1947,"He debuted for the Brooklyn Dodgers to become the first Black player in the modern major leagues. His number 42 is now retired across every team in the sport.")
A.lwP=new B.r('The London "Austerity Olympics" reopen the Games after the war',1948,"With no new venues built, athletes were housed in army camps and colleges. Dutch runner Fanny Blankers-Koen, a mother of two, won four sprint and hurdles golds.")
A.lAy=new B.r("Uruguay stun Brazil in the World Cup final at the Maracan\xe3",1950,"Around 200,000 fans packed the Rio stadium expecting a home party. The upset was so painful in Brazil that it earned its own name \u2014 the 'Maracanazo'.")
A.lu5=new B.r('West Germany win the World Cup in the "Miracle of Bern"',1954,"They beat a Hungary side unbeaten for four years, coming back from two goals down. The win is often credited with lifting national spirits in post-war Germany.")
A.lAl=new B.r("Melbourne hosts the first Olympics in the Southern Hemisphere",1956,"Australia's strict animal-quarantine rules meant the equestrian events had to be held thousands of miles away, in Stockholm, months earlier.")
A.lz5=new B.r("Brazil win their first World Cup as a 17-year-old Pel\xe9 emerges",1958,"Held in Sweden, it was the first World Cup broadcast to a wide television audience. Pel\xe9 scored twice in the final and wept on the pitch at the whistle.")
A.lxU=new B.r("Abebe Bikila wins the Rome Olympic marathon running barefoot",1960,"His race shoes didn't fit, so he ran the whole thing without any. He became the first athlete from sub-Saharan Africa to win an Olympic gold medal.")
A.lzU=new B.r("Brazil win a second straight football World Cup",1962,"Held in Chile, the tournament went ahead just two years after a huge earthquake had struck the host nation. Garrincha starred as Pel\xe9 was injured early on.")
A.lvt=new B.r("Celtic become the first British club to win the European Cup",1967,"The 'Lisbon Lions' beat Inter Milan 2-1. Remarkably, every single player in the squad had been born within about 30 miles of Celtic Park in Glasgow.")
A.lAd=new B.r("Bob Beamon leaps 8.90m at the Mexico City Olympics",1968,"His long jump smashed the world record by more than half a metre and stood for 23 years. At the same Games, Dick Fosbury unveiled his backwards 'flop' high-jump style.")
A.lu8=new B.r('Billie Jean King beats Bobby Riggs in the "Battle of the Sexes"',1973,"The tennis match drew a crowd of 30,000 and an estimated 90 million TV viewers worldwide \u2014 one of the most-watched matches ever played.")
A.lux=new B.r('Muhammad Ali beats George Foreman in the "Rumble in the Jungle"',1974,"Staged in Kinshasa, Ali leaned on the ropes to tire the younger champion \u2014 a tactic he later nicknamed 'rope-a-dope'. Around a billion people followed the fight.")
A.lBQ=new B.r("The first Cricket World Cup is held, and West Indies win it",1975,"Every match lasted a full 60 overs a side and was played in whites with a red ball. The final at Lord's ran so late it finished under fading evening light.")
A.lCT=new B.r("Nadia Com\u0103neci scores the first perfect 10 in Olympic gymnastics",1976,"The Montreal scoreboards couldn't even display it \u2014 they weren't built for a 10.00, so her mark flashed up as '1.00'. She was just 14 years old.")
A.lu3=new B.r("Carl Lewis wins four golds at the Los Angeles Olympics",1984,"He matched Jesse Owens' 1936 feat in the same four events. These Games also turned a profit \u2014 rare for an Olympics \u2014 thanks to private sponsorship.")
A.lx_=new B.r("Boris Becker becomes the youngest-ever men's Wimbledon champion",1985,"Aged 17 and unseeded, the German had never won a top-level title before. His diving, all-action style earned him the nickname 'Boom Boom'.")
A.lB0=new B.r('Maradona\'s "Goal of the Century" lights up the World Cup',1986,"In the Mexico quarter-final against England he dribbled past five players from inside his own half. Argentina went on to lift the trophy days later.")
A.lv4=new B.r('Steffi Graf completes the tennis "Golden Slam"',1988,"She won all four Grand Slam titles in one year and added Olympic gold in Seoul \u2014 a clean sweep no player, man or woman, had ever managed before.")
A.lBi=new B.r("Brazil win the football World Cup hosted in the United States",1994,"It was the first final ever decided by a penalty shootout, and Brazil's fourth world title. The tournament drew the highest average match attendance in World Cup history.")
A.lyx=new B.r("South Africa win the Rugby World Cup on home soil",1995,"Nelson Mandela handed the trophy to captain Francois Pienaar while wearing a Springbok jersey \u2014 a moment credited with helping unite the country. The film Invictus retold it.")
A.lyH=new B.r("France win their first football World Cup, as hosts",1998,"They beat Brazil 3-0 in the final in Paris, with Zinedine Zidane scoring twice with his head. It was the last World Cup with 32 teams before later expansions.")
A.lwL=new B.r("The United States win the Women's World Cup at a packed Rose Bowl",1999,"More than 90,000 fans watched the final \u2014 a record crowd for a women's sporting event. It was settled by a dramatic penalty shootout against China.")
A.lBm=new B.r("England win the Rugby World Cup with a last-minute drop goal",2003,"Jonny Wilkinson struck it with his weaker right foot in the final seconds of extra time against host nation Australia. It remains England's only rugby world title.")
A.lxk=new B.r("Italy win the football World Cup in Germany",2006,"The final against France went to penalties after 120 minutes. It was Italy's fourth world title, drawing them level with Germany at the time.")
A.lB_=new B.r("Usain Bolt runs 9.58 for the 100m at the Berlin World Championships",2009,"It shaved more than a tenth off his own record \u2014 a huge margin over such a short distance. More than 15 years on, no one has run faster.")
A.lwQ=new B.r("Spain win their first football World Cup, in South Africa",2010,"It was the first World Cup held on the African continent, its soundtrack the constant buzz of vuvuzela horns. Spain won every knockout match 1-0.")
A.lwr=new B.r("Germany win the World Cup in Brazil",2014,"They famously beat the host nation 7-1 in the semi-final \u2014 one of the most shocking scorelines in the tournament's history \u2014 before edging Argentina in the final.")
A.lyi=new B.r("New Zealand become the first team to retain the Rugby World Cup",2015,"The All Blacks, who perform the haka before every match, won back -to-back titles four years apart. The final was played at Twickenham in London.")
A.lyk=new B.r("Rio de Janeiro hosts the first Olympics held in South America",2016,"The Games opened on Copacabana's doorstep, with the marathon and cycling routes weaving past the city's beaches and mountains for a global television audience.")
A.lAX=new B.r("The Tokyo Olympics are held in near-empty stadiums",2021,"Postponed a year and staged largely without spectators, they were the first Games where medallists placed their own medals around their necks.")
A.lzJ=new B.r("Argentina win the football World Cup in Qatar",2022,"The final against France is widely called one of the greatest ever, decided on penalties after a 3-3 draw. It was the first World Cup held in the Middle East.")
A.lxn=new B.r("Paris opens its Olympics with a ceremony along the River Seine",2024,"Instead of a stadium parade, athletes floated downriver on boats past the city's landmarks \u2014 the first Summer Games opening held outside a stadium.")
